INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$631a1b64$SATQ$, $SATQ$1af032f6-cab8-4e18-889f-5bf400804724$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Since the Hubble Space Telescope was launched into space in 1990, astronauts have needed to complete regular missions to repair the telescope and keep it working smoothly. Researchers hope that robots will soon be able to make these repairs. Employing robots instead of humans to make repairs will be helpful, as <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> astronauts to maintain the telescope can be expensive.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d0e31730-2ab7-45ff-9661-ba699c1f19e4","content":"<p>straightening</p>"},{"id":"c3f37f89-4c13-4ac7-93fb-f4ce5f00e754","content":"<p>forgetting about</p>"},{"id":"90c37997-2426-4398-9297-d844763c90ed","content":"<p>relying on</p>"},{"id":"41c59eaa-b542-4d44-a598-532b78607bbb","content":"<p>reducing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["90c37997-2426-4398-9297-d844763c90ed"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of repairing the Hubble Space Telescope. In this context, &ldquo;relying on&rdquo; means depending on. The text states that astronauts have regularly had to go on missions to repair the Hubble Space Telescope but that robots might soon be able to make the repairs, which would be helpful because involving astronauts in the process is expensive. It is most reasonable to assume that it is the need to send astronauts on missions that is expensive; thus, the context suggests that employing robots for repairs will be helpful because depending on repeatedly sending humans to space is costly.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;straightening&rdquo; would mean physically aligning or removing curves and bends. It&rsquo;s not clear how astronauts would be physically straightened to maintain the telescope or why that would be expensive. Choice B is incorrect because the text suggests that it is the ongoing involvement of astronauts&mdash;the need to send them to space regularly&mdash;that is expensive. Nothing in the text indicates that astronauts have been forgotten about or that forgetting about them would be expensive. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;reducing&rdquo; would most likely mean lowering in status, and nothing in the text suggests that astronauts are diminished in any way by repairing the telescope (indeed, they have regularly done repairs for years). Further, it isn&rsquo;t clear how a decrease in status would be expensive.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"631a1b64","external_id":"1af032f6-cab8-4e18-889f-5bf400804724","vaultid":null,"uId":"5d4b8cf4-375f-499d-a239-e68d31be50e2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Since the Hubble Space Telescope was launched into space in 1990, astronauts have needed to complete regular missions to repair the telescope and keep it working smoothly. Researchers hope that robots will soon be able to make these repairs. Employing robots instead of humans to make repairs will be helpful, as <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> astronauts to maintain the telescope can be expensive.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d0e31730-2ab7-45ff-9661-ba699c1f19e4","content":"<p>straightening</p>"},{"id":"c3f37f89-4c13-4ac7-93fb-f4ce5f00e754","content":"<p>forgetting about</p>"},{"id":"90c37997-2426-4398-9297-d844763c90ed","content":"<p>relying on</p>"},{"id":"41c59eaa-b542-4d44-a598-532b78607bbb","content":"<p>reducing</p>"}],"keys":["90c37997-2426-4398-9297-d844763c90ed"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of repairing the Hubble Space Telescope. In this context, &ldquo;relying on&rdquo; means depending on. The text states that astronauts have regularly had to go on missions to repair the Hubble Space Telescope but that robots might soon be able to make the repairs, which would be helpful because involving astronauts in the process is expensive. It is most reasonable to assume that it is the need to send astronauts on missions that is expensive; thus, the context suggests that employing robots for repairs will be helpful because depending on repeatedly sending humans to space is costly.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;straightening&rdquo; would mean physically aligning or removing curves and bends. It&rsquo;s not clear how astronauts would be physically straightened to maintain the telescope or why that would be expensive. Choice B is incorrect because the text suggests that it is the ongoing involvement of astronauts&mdash;the need to send them to space regularly&mdash;that is expensive. Nothing in the text indicates that astronauts have been forgotten about or that forgetting about them would be expensive. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;reducing&rdquo; would most likely mean lowering in status, and nothing in the text suggests that astronauts are diminished in any way by repairing the telescope (indeed, they have regularly done repairs for years). Further, it isn&rsquo;t clear how a decrease in status would be expensive.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771167,"updateDate":1776362771167}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771167, 1776362771167),
    ($SATQ$637d0878$SATQ$, $SATQ$c28bc354-77cf-412b-a34b-d9fd49dfb362$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0c1acd6d-348b-4ba5-b81c-bdceae0b8543$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Appalachian Trail is a hiking path in the eastern United States. Much of the 2,000 mile trail passes through wilderness areas. In order to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> those areas, the United States Congress passed the National Trails System Act in 1968, ensuring that the trail would not be sold or commercially developed.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"081cc4f9-3d64-449b-82ef-fcbd9dde951f","content":"<p>borrow</p>"},{"id":"ab0dd193-d97e-47b2-a165-fa20c34a91a7","content":"<p>postpone</p>"},{"id":"4ab2abce-0773-48e3-bc8d-33402bbfca38","content":"<p>protect</p>"},{"id":"f1d5bc47-e7ee-44bf-89af-ef8eda69f18b","content":"<p>decorate</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4ab2abce-0773-48e3-bc8d-33402bbfca38"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Protect" means "preserve" or "keep safe from." By ensuring that the wilderness areas along the Appalachian Trail can&rsquo;t be sold or developed, the National Trails System Act protects them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "Borrow" means "to take something with intent to return it." The text doesn&rsquo;t say anything about taking and returning the wilderness that surrounds the Appalachian Trail. Choice B is incorrect. "Postpone" means "to put off until later." Nothing in the passage suggests that Congress wants to "postpone" the wilderness areas (and that doesn&rsquo;t make sense anyway&mdash;they can postpone doing something to the wilderness areas, but they can&rsquo;t postpone the areas themselves). Choice D is incorrect. "Decorate" means "to adorn" or "add extra items or pictures to make more attractive." No details in the text suggest that Congress wishes to make the trail fancier or more attractive.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-E008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bd035256-5dad-4ae4-873e-1fcff85c436e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"637d0878","external_id":"c28bc354-77cf-412b-a34b-d9fd49dfb362","vaultid":"0c1acd6d-348b-4ba5-b81c-bdceae0b8543","uId":"487aa232-6a31-45d6-b647-049da93df7b4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Appalachian Trail is a hiking path in the eastern United States. Much of the 2,000 mile trail passes through wilderness areas. In order to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> those areas, the United States Congress passed the National Trails System Act in 1968, ensuring that the trail would not be sold or commercially developed.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"081cc4f9-3d64-449b-82ef-fcbd9dde951f","content":"<p>borrow</p>"},{"id":"ab0dd193-d97e-47b2-a165-fa20c34a91a7","content":"<p>postpone</p>"},{"id":"4ab2abce-0773-48e3-bc8d-33402bbfca38","content":"<p>protect</p>"},{"id":"f1d5bc47-e7ee-44bf-89af-ef8eda69f18b","content":"<p>decorate</p>"}],"keys":["4ab2abce-0773-48e3-bc8d-33402bbfca38"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Protect\" means \"preserve\" or \"keep safe from.\" By ensuring that the wilderness areas along the Appalachian Trail can&rsquo;t be sold or developed, the National Trails System Act protects them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"Borrow\" means \"to take something with intent to return it.\" The text doesn&rsquo;t say anything about taking and returning the wilderness that surrounds the Appalachian Trail. Choice B is incorrect. \"Postpone\" means \"to put off until later.\" Nothing in the passage suggests that Congress wants to \"postpone\" the wilderness areas (and that doesn&rsquo;t make sense anyway&mdash;they can postpone doing something to the wilderness areas, but they can&rsquo;t postpone the areas themselves). Choice D is incorrect. \"Decorate\" means \"to adorn\" or \"add extra items or pictures to make more attractive.\" No details in the text suggest that Congress wishes to make the trail fancier or more attractive.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-E008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bd035256-5dad-4ae4-873e-1fcff85c436e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$65406d2c$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c299e65-5cc8-407b-a73f-f13b6f6b5576$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Dubautia carrii</em> is a species in a family of plants known collectively as the silversword alliance, all of which grow only on the Hawaiian Islands. Members of this alliance exhibit an extraordinary range of phenotypes, with some species maturing into vines and others into shrubs and trees. All species in the alliance descended from a single ancestral tarweed plant that arrived on the islands around 5 million years ago. The tarweed&rsquo;s descendants diversified into distinct species as they adapted to live in the wide variety of habitats found on the Hawaiian Islands.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d3c15424-3269-4237-98d7-6383eab6f7e1","content":"<p>To indicate the specific tarweed ancestor of all plants that grow on the Hawaiian Islands and explain why the plants have such varied physical characteristics</p>"},{"id":"162fe182-9fe2-42ab-b201-c85a16d93b66","content":"<p>To describe the specific habitat where <em>Dubautia carrii</em> are found and identify other plants that share a common ancestor with them</p>"},{"id":"ebcbd652-3102-4a33-9325-c7aaa5908fa5","content":"<p>To describe the silversword alliance and explain how the plant family became so varied</p>"},{"id":"92f4e15b-da14-423c-b4ae-0e11101c014c","content":"<p>To advance the claim that all plants on the Hawaiian Islands are part of the silversword alliance and list possible ancestors of the plants that make up the alliance</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ebcbd652-3102-4a33-9325-c7aaa5908fa5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to describe the family of plants known as the silversword alliance and explain why the plants came to exhibit "an extraordinary range of phenotypes." The text introduces the silversword alliance as a family of plants unique to the Hawaiian Islands, presenting those that mature into vines and those that mature into shrubs and trees as examples of different phenotypes. The text concludes by explaining that these plants are all descendants of "a single ancestral tarweed plant" that arrived 5 million years ago in the Hawaiian Islands, where a "wide variety of habitats" drove the plant&rsquo;s descendants to adapt and thus differentiate themselves, diversifying into a group of related but distinct species with different physical characteristics.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text explains why the plants within the silversword alliance have varied physical characteristics, the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that tarweed is the ancestor of all plants on the Hawaiian Islands. The text states instead that all members of the silversword alliance descend from a single tarweed ancestor. Choice B is incorrect because while <em>Dubautia carrii</em> is mentioned as an example of a species in the silversword alliance, no details about its particular habitat are provided. And although the text indicates that the Hawaiian Islands are home to "a wide variety of habitats," it doesn&rsquo;t describe the specific habitat where <em>Dubautia carrii</em> is found. The text discusses the silversword alliance as a whole rather than focusing on <em>Dubautia carrii</em> or any one species within this family. Choice D is incorrect. Although the silversword alliance is only found on the Hawaiian Islands and includes "an extraordinary range of phenotypes," the text doesn&rsquo;t claim that all plants on the Hawaiian Islands are part of the alliance. Furthermore, the text indicates that these plants all "descended from a single ancestral tarweed plant," not that there are multiple possible candidates for that ancestor plant.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"65406d2c","external_id":"5c299e65-5cc8-407b-a73f-f13b6f6b5576","vaultid":null,"uId":"5de42b6f-7064-4a4b-b0bc-05112104d99d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Dubautia carrii</em> is a species in a family of plants known collectively as the silversword alliance, all of which grow only on the Hawaiian Islands. Members of this alliance exhibit an extraordinary range of phenotypes, with some species maturing into vines and others into shrubs and trees. All species in the alliance descended from a single ancestral tarweed plant that arrived on the islands around 5 million years ago. The tarweed&rsquo;s descendants diversified into distinct species as they adapted to live in the wide variety of habitats found on the Hawaiian Islands.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d3c15424-3269-4237-98d7-6383eab6f7e1","content":"<p>To indicate the specific tarweed ancestor of all plants that grow on the Hawaiian Islands and explain why the plants have such varied physical characteristics</p>"},{"id":"162fe182-9fe2-42ab-b201-c85a16d93b66","content":"<p>To describe the specific habitat where <em>Dubautia carrii</em> are found and identify other plants that share a common ancestor with them</p>"},{"id":"ebcbd652-3102-4a33-9325-c7aaa5908fa5","content":"<p>To describe the silversword alliance and explain how the plant family became so varied</p>"},{"id":"92f4e15b-da14-423c-b4ae-0e11101c014c","content":"<p>To advance the claim that all plants on the Hawaiian Islands are part of the silversword alliance and list possible ancestors of the plants that make up the alliance</p>"}],"keys":["ebcbd652-3102-4a33-9325-c7aaa5908fa5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to describe the family of plants known as the silversword alliance and explain why the plants came to exhibit \"an extraordinary range of phenotypes.\" The text introduces the silversword alliance as a family of plants unique to the Hawaiian Islands, presenting those that mature into vines and those that mature into shrubs and trees as examples of different phenotypes. The text concludes by explaining that these plants are all descendants of \"a single ancestral tarweed plant\" that arrived 5 million years ago in the Hawaiian Islands, where a \"wide variety of habitats\" drove the plant&rsquo;s descendants to adapt and thus differentiate themselves, diversifying into a group of related but distinct species with different physical characteristics.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text explains why the plants within the silversword alliance have varied physical characteristics, the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that tarweed is the ancestor of all plants on the Hawaiian Islands. The text states instead that all members of the silversword alliance descend from a single tarweed ancestor. Choice B is incorrect because while <em>Dubautia carrii</em> is mentioned as an example of a species in the silversword alliance, no details about its particular habitat are provided. And although the text indicates that the Hawaiian Islands are home to \"a wide variety of habitats,\" it doesn&rsquo;t describe the specific habitat where <em>Dubautia carrii</em> is found. The text discusses the silversword alliance as a whole rather than focusing on <em>Dubautia carrii</em> or any one species within this family. Choice D is incorrect. Although the silversword alliance is only found on the Hawaiian Islands and includes \"an extraordinary range of phenotypes,\" the text doesn&rsquo;t claim that all plants on the Hawaiian Islands are part of the alliance. Furthermore, the text indicates that these plants all \"descended from a single ancestral tarweed plant,\" not that there are multiple possible candidates for that ancestor plant.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061177,"updateDate":1755117061177}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061177, 1755117061177),
    ($SATQ$662892ad$SATQ$, $SATQ$1de2c5d1-3cf4-4e47-9172-c03d89d88880$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Wakako Yamauchi is best known for <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em>, her 1977 play about a Japanese American family in Southern California. The play is based on a short story Yamauchi had published three years earlier. Adapting the story wasn&rsquo;t easy. Theater relies on dialogue between characters, but the original story features little dialogue and instead describes its characters&rsquo; silent thoughts. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">To transform the story into a play, Yamauchi created situations where characters reveal their thoughts by speaking them aloud during conversations with each other.</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1fd87098-53a7-4553-886e-2da678f11906","content":"<p>It offers information about how Yamauchi adapted her short story into a play.</p>"},{"id":"a96be5fa-1ad2-45b0-8316-2e9a32e46cad","content":"<p>It argues that Yamauchi&rsquo;s play influenced later playwrights.</p>"},{"id":"98d7a5ec-a37a-4163-bbcb-5c86ddfe14e0","content":"<p>It explains why Yamauchi&rsquo;s short story is better known than the play adaptation is.</p>"},{"id":"5a34a004-b908-4da3-a44c-fb845bdd0be0","content":"<p>It describes how Yamauchi chose the actors who performed in the play.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1fd87098-53a7-4553-886e-2da678f11906"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. According to the text, it was hard for Yamauchi to adapt her short story into the play <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em> because the story had included little dialogue, instead describing the characters&rsquo; silent thoughts. The underlined sentence offers information about how Yamauchi ultimately succeeded in adapting the story into a play: it explains that Yamauchi created situations where the characters of the play could reveal their internal thoughts while speaking with each other.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole makes any mention of playwrights other than Yamauchi or how the play might have influenced them. Choice C is incorrect because the text states that Yamauchi is best known for her play <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em>, which conveys that the play is better known than the short story it&rsquo;s based on is. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole mentions the actors who performed in the play or Yamauchi&rsquo;s approach to choosing them.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"662892ad","external_id":"1de2c5d1-3cf4-4e47-9172-c03d89d88880","vaultid":null,"uId":"d35de12c-c36a-4170-bf0b-a7b48ef2ab13","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Wakako Yamauchi is best known for <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em>, her 1977 play about a Japanese American family in Southern California. The play is based on a short story Yamauchi had published three years earlier. Adapting the story wasn&rsquo;t easy. Theater relies on dialogue between characters, but the original story features little dialogue and instead describes its characters&rsquo; silent thoughts. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">To transform the story into a play, Yamauchi created situations where characters reveal their thoughts by speaking them aloud during conversations with each other.</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1fd87098-53a7-4553-886e-2da678f11906","content":"<p>It offers information about how Yamauchi adapted her short story into a play.</p>"},{"id":"a96be5fa-1ad2-45b0-8316-2e9a32e46cad","content":"<p>It argues that Yamauchi&rsquo;s play influenced later playwrights.</p>"},{"id":"98d7a5ec-a37a-4163-bbcb-5c86ddfe14e0","content":"<p>It explains why Yamauchi&rsquo;s short story is better known than the play adaptation is.</p>"},{"id":"5a34a004-b908-4da3-a44c-fb845bdd0be0","content":"<p>It describes how Yamauchi chose the actors who performed in the play.</p>"}],"keys":["1fd87098-53a7-4553-886e-2da678f11906"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. According to the text, it was hard for Yamauchi to adapt her short story into the play <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em> because the story had included little dialogue, instead describing the characters&rsquo; silent thoughts. The underlined sentence offers information about how Yamauchi ultimately succeeded in adapting the story into a play: it explains that Yamauchi created situations where the characters of the play could reveal their internal thoughts while speaking with each other.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole makes any mention of playwrights other than Yamauchi or how the play might have influenced them. Choice C is incorrect because the text states that Yamauchi is best known for her play <em>And the Soul Shall Dance</em>, which conveys that the play is better known than the short story it&rsquo;s based on is. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole mentions the actors who performed in the play or Yamauchi&rsquo;s approach to choosing them.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669203,"updateDate":1743430669203}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669203, 1743430669203),
    ($SATQ$662ebff2$SATQ$, $SATQ$0fb21e0f-9c50-4188-9584-7dc983430729$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In response to concerns that some recent financial crises were exacerbated by consumers misunderstanding risks associated with credit cards, loans, and other financial products, policymakers in many countries have instituted risk-disclosure requirements on sellers of those products. Enrique Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> investigated a variety of risk-disclosure messages sent to thousands of credit card customers and found that the messages had only small and short-lived effects on behavior. Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> asserted that such effects may nevertheless be worth pursuing, <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">given the negligible cost of messaging</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b541b29d-b2d0-4868-ad92-2a9720655e01","content":"<p>It notes a factor that led Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> to not dismiss risk-disclosure messaging altogether despite their evidence of its limited utility.</p>"},{"id":"19a94c3f-2dd6-44e9-9bd3-7c510e982001","content":"<p>It acknowledges a type of risk-disclosure messaging that Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> may not have fully accounted for in their study.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2d2c048c-1c3f-42d7-9883-12d1e7f85caf","content":"<p>It describes a consideration that explains why Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> recommended risk-disclosure messaging even though its effects may be small relative to its costs.</p>"},{"id":"98ab4622-f4ce-47d7-a291-9ac822ab065f","content":"<p>It points out a circumstance that Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> conceded may make risk-disclosure messaging more effective than their study suggests.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b541b29d-b2d0-4868-ad92-2a9720655e01"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole. The text establishes that many countries have adopted risk-disclosure requirements for financial products due to concerns that consumers don&rsquo;t understand the risks associated with the products. According to the text, Seira et al. found that the effects of such messaging on consumer behavior were small and temporary. The text then adds that the researchers assert that because the cost of the messaging is negligible, the approach may be worth doing even if the effects are limited. Thus, the underlined portion notes a factor&mdash;very low cost&mdash;that led the researchers to not completely dismiss risk-disclosure messaging despite their evidence of its limited utility.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t refer to a particular type of risk-disclosure messaging, whether Seira et al. considered it or not; the underlined portion simply indicates that the cost of the messaging (broadly) is very low, which makes the approach worth pursuing even if its effects are limited. Choice C is incorrect. Although the underlined portion does describe a consideration that led the researchers to recommend risk-disclosure messaging despite the messaging&rsquo;s small effects on consumer behavior, it directly states that the cost of such messaging is negligible, or very low&mdash;meaning that both the effects and the costs are small, not that the effects are small only relative to the costs. Choice D is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no indication that Seira et al. suggest that risk-disclosure messaging could be more effective if it had lower costs; rather, the underlined portion indicates that Seira et al. believe the already negligible cost of messaging makes the approach worth pursuing even if its effects are limited.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"662ebff2","external_id":"0fb21e0f-9c50-4188-9584-7dc983430729","vaultid":null,"uId":"1f2f2b52-c5d9-413f-8c35-2736973d9293","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In response to concerns that some recent financial crises were exacerbated by consumers misunderstanding risks associated with credit cards, loans, and other financial products, policymakers in many countries have instituted risk-disclosure requirements on sellers of those products. Enrique Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> investigated a variety of risk-disclosure messages sent to thousands of credit card customers and found that the messages had only small and short-lived effects on behavior. Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> asserted that such effects may nevertheless be worth pursuing, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">given the negligible cost of messaging</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b541b29d-b2d0-4868-ad92-2a9720655e01","content":"<p>It notes a factor that led Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> to not dismiss risk-disclosure messaging altogether despite their evidence of its limited utility.</p>"},{"id":"19a94c3f-2dd6-44e9-9bd3-7c510e982001","content":"<p>It acknowledges a type of risk-disclosure messaging that Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> may not have fully accounted for in their study.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2d2c048c-1c3f-42d7-9883-12d1e7f85caf","content":"<p>It describes a consideration that explains why Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> recommended risk-disclosure messaging even though its effects may be small relative to its costs.</p>"},{"id":"98ab4622-f4ce-47d7-a291-9ac822ab065f","content":"<p>It points out a circumstance that Seira <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> conceded may make risk-disclosure messaging more effective than their study suggests.</p>"}],"keys":["b541b29d-b2d0-4868-ad92-2a9720655e01"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole. The text establishes that many countries have adopted risk-disclosure requirements for financial products due to concerns that consumers don&rsquo;t understand the risks associated with the products. According to the text, Seira et al. found that the effects of such messaging on consumer behavior were small and temporary. The text then adds that the researchers assert that because the cost of the messaging is negligible, the approach may be worth doing even if the effects are limited. Thus, the underlined portion notes a factor&mdash;very low cost&mdash;that led the researchers to not completely dismiss risk-disclosure messaging despite their evidence of its limited utility.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t refer to a particular type of risk-disclosure messaging, whether Seira et al. considered it or not; the underlined portion simply indicates that the cost of the messaging (broadly) is very low, which makes the approach worth pursuing even if its effects are limited. Choice C is incorrect. Although the underlined portion does describe a consideration that led the researchers to recommend risk-disclosure messaging despite the messaging&rsquo;s small effects on consumer behavior, it directly states that the cost of such messaging is negligible, or very low&mdash;meaning that both the effects and the costs are small, not that the effects are small only relative to the costs. Choice D is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no indication that Seira et al. suggest that risk-disclosure messaging could be more effective if it had lower costs; rather, the underlined portion indicates that Seira et al. believe the already negligible cost of messaging makes the approach worth pursuing even if its effects are limited.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669203,"updateDate":1743430669203}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669203, 1743430669203),
    ($SATQ$683f66c9$SATQ$, $SATQ$7c28e20b-3062-42a5-bc4a-4f3e2a5a8f11$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Though not closely related, the hedgehog tenrecs of Madagascar share basic <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> true hedgehogs, including protective spines, pointed snouts, and small body size&mdash;traits the two groups of mammals independently developed in response to equivalent roles in their respective habitats.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"48983744-1cb8-4adc-ba6e-55c6e78cfae6","content":"<p>examples of</p>"},{"id":"c7d356f6-1ee6-4005-94d3-ddfca4062107","content":"<p>concerns about</p>"},{"id":"7dd5bea8-c9a3-47a1-8e12-cea38bc78140","content":"<p>indications of</p>"},{"id":"a804526e-8851-45b3-b1b2-00312db07e4a","content":"<p>similarities with</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a804526e-8851-45b3-b1b2-00312db07e4a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because in context, sharing "similarities with" means having some resemblance to. The text establishes a comparison between two groups of mammals, stating that although they aren&rsquo;t closely related, hedgehog tenrecs and true hedgehogs play similar roles in their habitats, a circumstance that has resulted in the independent development of some of the same physical traits. This context supports the idea that hedgehog tenrecs resemble, or share basic similarities with, true hedgehogs in some respects.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that hedgehog tenrecs are "examples of," or representative of, true hedgehogs. The text states that despite some shared physical traits, the two groups of mammals aren&rsquo;t closely related; therefore, hedgehog tenrecs can&rsquo;t be examples of true hedgehogs. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, "concerns about" would mean worries about or interests in. The text focuses on the various physical traits that hedgehog tenrecs and true hedgehogs share. There&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest why hedgehog tenrecs would be worried about true hedgehogs, or why they would be interested in them. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "indications of" would mean evidence of. By listing a set of traits that hedgehog tenrecs share with true hedgehogs, the text establishes a comparison between the two groups of mammals, and saying that the traits shared within one group of mammals provide evidence of another group of mammals wouldn&rsquo;t be an effective way to establish the similarities between the two groups.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"683f66c9","external_id":"7c28e20b-3062-42a5-bc4a-4f3e2a5a8f11","vaultid":null,"uId":"95828aba-6d27-4afd-82d2-af3e5b02c8ea","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Though not closely related, the hedgehog tenrecs of Madagascar share basic <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> true hedgehogs, including protective spines, pointed snouts, and small body size&mdash;traits the two groups of mammals independently developed in response to equivalent roles in their respective habitats.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"48983744-1cb8-4adc-ba6e-55c6e78cfae6","content":"<p>examples of</p>"},{"id":"c7d356f6-1ee6-4005-94d3-ddfca4062107","content":"<p>concerns about</p>"},{"id":"7dd5bea8-c9a3-47a1-8e12-cea38bc78140","content":"<p>indications of</p>"},{"id":"a804526e-8851-45b3-b1b2-00312db07e4a","content":"<p>similarities with</p>"}],"keys":["a804526e-8851-45b3-b1b2-00312db07e4a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because in context, sharing \"similarities with\" means having some resemblance to. The text establishes a comparison between two groups of mammals, stating that although they aren&rsquo;t closely related, hedgehog tenrecs and true hedgehogs play similar roles in their habitats, a circumstance that has resulted in the independent development of some of the same physical traits. This context supports the idea that hedgehog tenrecs resemble, or share basic similarities with, true hedgehogs in some respects.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that hedgehog tenrecs are \"examples of,\" or representative of, true hedgehogs. The text states that despite some shared physical traits, the two groups of mammals aren&rsquo;t closely related; therefore, hedgehog tenrecs can&rsquo;t be examples of true hedgehogs. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, \"concerns about\" would mean worries about or interests in. The text focuses on the various physical traits that hedgehog tenrecs and true hedgehogs share. There&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest why hedgehog tenrecs would be worried about true hedgehogs, or why they would be interested in them. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"indications of\" would mean evidence of. By listing a set of traits that hedgehog tenrecs share with true hedgehogs, the text establishes a comparison between the two groups of mammals, and saying that the traits shared within one group of mammals provide evidence of another group of mammals wouldn&rsquo;t be an effective way to establish the similarities between the two groups.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$6977d22b$SATQ$, $SATQ$68b05d6a-7ee1-4b36-962d-051b9396eb85$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$06204a7a-2ddb-4730-a86f-d9ffbd6b4af3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Ecologists have long wondered how thousands of microscopic phytoplankton species can live together near ocean surfaces competing for the same resources. According to <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>conventional wisdom</u></span>, one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest. So why do so many species remain? Ecologists&rsquo; many efforts to explain this phenomenon still haven&rsquo;t uncovered a satisfactory explanation.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Ecologist Michael Behrenfeld and colleagues have connected phytoplankton&rsquo;s diversity to their microscopic size. Because these organisms are so tiny, they are spaced relatively far apart from each other in ocean water and, moreover, experience that water as a relatively dense substance. This in turn makes it hard for them to move around and interact with one another. Therefore, says Behrenfeld&rsquo;s team, direct competition among phytoplankton probably happens much less than previously thought.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would Behrenfeld and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the &ldquo;conventional wisdom&rdquo; discussed in Text 1? </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"63a2e6a0-ac79-49f3-91b3-0baa9231ce91","content":"<p>By arguing that it is based on a misconception about phytoplankton species competing with one another</p>"},{"id":"84f3244f-9dab-49d9-a09f-0f0280ffc3fc","content":"<p>By asserting that it fails to recognize that routine replenishment of ocean nutrients prevents competition between phytoplankton species&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9c989d00-f875-4c7c-af5e-7770085be65d","content":"<p>By suggesting that their own findings help clarify how phytoplankton species are able to compete with larger organisms</p>"},{"id":"838c3d54-404a-490b-a89e-362a51fec911","content":"<p>By recommending that more ecologists focus their research on how competition among phytoplankton species is increased with water density</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["63a2e6a0-ac79-49f3-91b3-0baa9231ce91"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because based on Text 2, it represents how Behrenfeld and colleagues would most likely respond to the &ldquo;conventional wisdom&rdquo; discussed in Text 1. The conventional wisdom cited holds the opinion that when there is species diversity within a phytoplankton population, &ldquo;one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest&rdquo;&mdash;that is, after being so successful in competing for resources that the other species vanish from the population. However, Text 2 explains that according to Behrenfeld and colleagues, phytoplankton are so small and spaced so far apart in the water that there is &ldquo;much less&rdquo; direct competition for resources within phytoplankton populations than scientists had previously thought.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because Text 2 never discusses whether routine replenishment of ocean nutrients affects competition between phytoplankton species. Choice C is incorrect because the interspecies competition discussed in both texts is specifically between phytoplankton species, and neither text considers whether phytoplankton compete for resources with larger nonphytoplankton species.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because according to Text 2, Behrenfeld and colleagues argue that water density decreases, not increases, competition between phytoplankton species. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-004-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0c2e918b-ecc0-488e-9bda-c638a14b7fb7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6977d22b","external_id":"68b05d6a-7ee1-4b36-962d-051b9396eb85","vaultid":"06204a7a-2ddb-4730-a86f-d9ffbd6b4af3","uId":"e841acd2-eaf0-4bdd-aa5c-71043911c78d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Ecologists have long wondered how thousands of microscopic phytoplankton species can live together near ocean surfaces competing for the same resources. According to <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>conventional wisdom</u></span>, one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest. So why do so many species remain? Ecologists&rsquo; many efforts to explain this phenomenon still haven&rsquo;t uncovered a satisfactory explanation.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Ecologist Michael Behrenfeld and colleagues have connected phytoplankton&rsquo;s diversity to their microscopic size. Because these organisms are so tiny, they are spaced relatively far apart from each other in ocean water and, moreover, experience that water as a relatively dense substance. This in turn makes it hard for them to move around and interact with one another. Therefore, says Behrenfeld&rsquo;s team, direct competition among phytoplankton probably happens much less than previously thought.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would Behrenfeld and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the &ldquo;conventional wisdom&rdquo; discussed in Text 1? </p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"63a2e6a0-ac79-49f3-91b3-0baa9231ce91","content":"<p>By arguing that it is based on a misconception about phytoplankton species competing with one another</p>"},{"id":"84f3244f-9dab-49d9-a09f-0f0280ffc3fc","content":"<p>By asserting that it fails to recognize that routine replenishment of ocean nutrients prevents competition between phytoplankton species&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9c989d00-f875-4c7c-af5e-7770085be65d","content":"<p>By suggesting that their own findings help clarify how phytoplankton species are able to compete with larger organisms</p>"},{"id":"838c3d54-404a-490b-a89e-362a51fec911","content":"<p>By recommending that more ecologists focus their research on how competition among phytoplankton species is increased with water density</p>"}],"keys":["63a2e6a0-ac79-49f3-91b3-0baa9231ce91"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because based on Text 2, it represents how Behrenfeld and colleagues would most likely respond to the &ldquo;conventional wisdom&rdquo; discussed in Text 1. The conventional wisdom cited holds the opinion that when there is species diversity within a phytoplankton population, &ldquo;one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest&rdquo;&mdash;that is, after being so successful in competing for resources that the other species vanish from the population. However, Text 2 explains that according to Behrenfeld and colleagues, phytoplankton are so small and spaced so far apart in the water that there is &ldquo;much less&rdquo; direct competition for resources within phytoplankton populations than scientists had previously thought.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because Text 2 never discusses whether routine replenishment of ocean nutrients affects competition between phytoplankton species. Choice C is incorrect because the interspecies competition discussed in both texts is specifically between phytoplankton species, and neither text considers whether phytoplankton compete for resources with larger nonphytoplankton species.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because according to Text 2, Behrenfeld and colleagues argue that water density decreases, not increases, competition between phytoplankton species. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-004-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0c2e918b-ecc0-488e-9bda-c638a14b7fb7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959312,"updateDate":1691007959312}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959312, 1691007959312),
    ($SATQ$69a6d050$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c87b72d-e67b-4861-bd6c-49c722059c99$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ae978922-c3e6-4b41-a284-50c3c7175a14$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the early 1800s, the Cherokee scholar Sequoyah created the first script, or writing system, for an Indigenous language in the United States. Because it represented the sounds of spoken Cherokee so accurately, his script was easy to learn and thus quickly achieved <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> use: by 1830, over 90 percent of the Cherokee people could read and write it.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"56896059-82bb-441a-8009-7c15c7122d9b","content":"<p>widespread</p>"},{"id":"d9f5af1e-dd68-46ed-a755-dbdbc703db2e","content":"<p>careful</p>"},{"id":"67f44715-acb7-4520-ae75-044a708a1b6e","content":"<p>unintended</p>"},{"id":"87cd2369-5cb8-4487-89de-36013382780f","content":"<p>infrequent</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["56896059-82bb-441a-8009-7c15c7122d9b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the writing system created by Sequoyah. In this context, &ldquo;widespread&rdquo; means widely accepted or practiced. The text indicates that because Sequoyah&rsquo;s script accurately represented the spoken sounds of the Cherokee language and was easy to learn, nearly all Cherokee people were able to read and write it soon after it was created. This context demonstrates that the script was widely used by the Cherokee people. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;careful&rdquo; would mean exercised with care and attentive concern. Although the work of creating a writing system likely involved great care, the text indicates that the system was &ldquo;easy to learn,&rdquo; which conflicts with the idea that using this system requires a noteworthy amount of care. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;unintended&rdquo; means not deliberate. The idea that using Sequoyah&rsquo;s script was unintentional conflicts directly with the claim that it was easy to learn and used by &ldquo;over 90% of the Cherokee people&rdquo; by 1830. In fact, because one had to learn this system, it&rsquo;s not clear how one could use it unintentionally.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;infrequent&rdquo; means rare or not occurring often, which conflicts directly with the claim that &ldquo;over 90% of the Cherokee people&rdquo; were using Sequoyah&rsquo;s script by 1830. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-973-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b7ff5eb8-1c7c-49ed-9d2e-10a5932a18cb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"69a6d050","external_id":"5c87b72d-e67b-4861-bd6c-49c722059c99","vaultid":"ae978922-c3e6-4b41-a284-50c3c7175a14","uId":"28f0debd-7227-4f97-a465-bc7e88859f0c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the early 1800s, the Cherokee scholar Sequoyah created the first script, or writing system, for an Indigenous language in the United States. Because it represented the sounds of spoken Cherokee so accurately, his script was easy to learn and thus quickly achieved <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> use: by 1830, over 90 percent of the Cherokee people could read and write it.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"56896059-82bb-441a-8009-7c15c7122d9b","content":"<p>widespread</p>"},{"id":"d9f5af1e-dd68-46ed-a755-dbdbc703db2e","content":"<p>careful</p>"},{"id":"67f44715-acb7-4520-ae75-044a708a1b6e","content":"<p>unintended</p>"},{"id":"87cd2369-5cb8-4487-89de-36013382780f","content":"<p>infrequent</p>"}],"keys":["56896059-82bb-441a-8009-7c15c7122d9b"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the writing system created by Sequoyah. In this context, &ldquo;widespread&rdquo; means widely accepted or practiced. The text indicates that because Sequoyah&rsquo;s script accurately represented the spoken sounds of the Cherokee language and was easy to learn, nearly all Cherokee people were able to read and write it soon after it was created. This context demonstrates that the script was widely used by the Cherokee people. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;careful&rdquo; would mean exercised with care and attentive concern. Although the work of creating a writing system likely involved great care, the text indicates that the system was &ldquo;easy to learn,&rdquo; which conflicts with the idea that using this system requires a noteworthy amount of care. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;unintended&rdquo; means not deliberate. The idea that using Sequoyah&rsquo;s script was unintentional conflicts directly with the claim that it was easy to learn and used by &ldquo;over 90% of the Cherokee people&rdquo; by 1830. In fact, because one had to learn this system, it&rsquo;s not clear how one could use it unintentionally.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;infrequent&rdquo; means rare or not occurring often, which conflicts directly with the claim that &ldquo;over 90% of the Cherokee people&rdquo; were using Sequoyah&rsquo;s script by 1830. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-973-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b7ff5eb8-1c7c-49ed-9d2e-10a5932a18cb","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$6a1194e8$SATQ$, $SATQ$e350387d-e07d-4cbb-aff6-846184c9dc92$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6993fa5b-03ce-42a9-b064-51132e09d661$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Rydra Wong, the protagonist of Samuel R. Delany&rsquo;s 1966 novel <em>Babel-17</em>, is a poet, an occupation which, in Delany&rsquo;s work, is not <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: nearly a dozen of the characters that populate his novels are poets or writers.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"03850d8d-13ee-4e47-9d72-b4154b012a21","content":"<p>infallible</p>"},{"id":"2528d221-6657-4215-917e-1f8f4d47985e","content":"<p>atypical</p>"},{"id":"6f070f1a-f5e6-4ff5-89a4-a0b7e992ac77","content":"<p>lucrative</p>"},{"id":"a52780da-0937-47a3-92b0-bf1a1ae19234","content":"<p>tedious</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2528d221-6657-4215-917e-1f8f4d47985e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Samuel R. Delany&rsquo;s character Rydra Wong. As used in this context, &ldquo;atypical&rdquo; would mean unrepresentative or not common. The text indicates that Wong is one of &ldquo;nearly a dozen&rdquo; characters in Delany&rsquo;s novels who are poets or writers. This context conveys that being a poet isn&rsquo;t an atypical occupation for a character in one of Delany&rsquo;s works. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;infallible&rdquo; means to be accurate or without fault, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the fact that Delany has written many characters who are poets and writers. This context suggests that the occupation isn&rsquo;t atypical for Delany, not that the occupation isn&rsquo;t infallible, or problematic.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;lucrative&rdquo; means to be profitable, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. If writing poet characters weren&rsquo;t profitable, it wouldn&rsquo;t be logical to explain this by citing that Delany gave many of his characters the same occupation.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;tedious&rdquo; means to be boring, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the fact that Delany has written many characters who are poets and writers. This context suggests that the occupation isn&rsquo;t atypical for Delany, not that the occupation isn&rsquo;t tedious. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-925-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0cb0d687-a000-4373-a615-7872ebfba65d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6a1194e8","external_id":"e350387d-e07d-4cbb-aff6-846184c9dc92","vaultid":"6993fa5b-03ce-42a9-b064-51132e09d661","uId":"d8224fde-312b-48f7-b1b6-26cbb82e36b1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Rydra Wong, the protagonist of Samuel R. Delany&rsquo;s 1966 novel <em>Babel-17</em>, is a poet, an occupation which, in Delany&rsquo;s work, is not <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: nearly a dozen of the characters that populate his novels are poets or writers.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"03850d8d-13ee-4e47-9d72-b4154b012a21","content":"<p>infallible</p>"},{"id":"2528d221-6657-4215-917e-1f8f4d47985e","content":"<p>atypical</p>"},{"id":"6f070f1a-f5e6-4ff5-89a4-a0b7e992ac77","content":"<p>lucrative</p>"},{"id":"a52780da-0937-47a3-92b0-bf1a1ae19234","content":"<p>tedious</p>"}],"keys":["2528d221-6657-4215-917e-1f8f4d47985e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Samuel R. Delany&rsquo;s character Rydra Wong. As used in this context, &ldquo;atypical&rdquo; would mean unrepresentative or not common. The text indicates that Wong is one of &ldquo;nearly a dozen&rdquo; characters in Delany&rsquo;s novels who are poets or writers. This context conveys that being a poet isn&rsquo;t an atypical occupation for a character in one of Delany&rsquo;s works. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;infallible&rdquo; means to be accurate or without fault, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the fact that Delany has written many characters who are poets and writers. This context suggests that the occupation isn&rsquo;t atypical for Delany, not that the occupation isn&rsquo;t infallible, or problematic.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;lucrative&rdquo; means to be profitable, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. If writing poet characters weren&rsquo;t profitable, it wouldn&rsquo;t be logical to explain this by citing that Delany gave many of his characters the same occupation.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;tedious&rdquo; means to be boring, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the fact that Delany has written many characters who are poets and writers. This context suggests that the occupation isn&rsquo;t atypical for Delany, not that the occupation isn&rsquo;t tedious. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-925-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0cb0d687-a000-4373-a615-7872ebfba65d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959355,"updateDate":1691007959355}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959355, 1691007959355),
    ($SATQ$6a1dc7c5$SATQ$, $SATQ$d149ddec-9f97-4f22-a5b6-683f4090e8b9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2e3d15df-e144-45c4-97e9-52dfe2cc49f6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Virginia Woolf&rsquo;s 1928 novel <em>Orlando </em>is an oddity within her body of work. Her other major novels consist mainly of scenes of everyday life and describe their characters&rsquo; interior states in great detail, whereas <em>Orlando </em>propels itself through a series of fantastical events and considers its characters&rsquo; psychology more superficially. Woolf herself sometimes regarded the novel as a minor work, even admitting once that she &ldquo;began it as a joke.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Like Woolf&rsquo;s other great novels, <em>Orlando </em>portrays how people&rsquo;s memories inform their experience of the present. Like those works, it examines how people navigate social interactions shaped by gender and social class. Though it is lighter in tone&mdash;more entertaining, even&mdash;this literary &ldquo;joke&rdquo; nonetheless engages seriously with the themes that motivated the four or five other novels by Woolf that have achieved the status of literary classics.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the assessment of <em>Orlando </em>presented in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d707e9b6-c4cf-4952-b6d3-1b4143be8765","content":"<p>By conceding that Woolf&rsquo;s talents were best suited to serious novels but asserting that the humor in <em>Orlando </em>is often effective</p>"},{"id":"b8629449-dd1d-4a1c-ada3-a93c0a586252","content":"<p>By agreeing that <em>Orlando</em> is less impressive than certain other novels by Woolf but arguing that it should still be regarded as a classic</p>"},{"id":"c1ee30a0-9757-4740-875c-b01f2a00b354","content":"<p>By acknowledging that <em>Orlando</em> clearly differs from Woolf&rsquo;s other major novels but insisting on its centrality to her body of work nonetheless&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"88822456-967f-412a-99c2-ef9c49517b0b","content":"<p>By concurring that the reputation of <em>Orlando</em> as a minor work has led readers to overlook this novel but maintaining that the reputation is unearned&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c1ee30a0-9757-4740-875c-b01f2a00b354"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the assessment of <em>Orlando </em>in Text 1. Both authors agree that <em>Orlando </em>is unusual for Woolf: Text 1 states that the novel examines its characters&rsquo; psychologies more superficially than Woolf&rsquo;s other novels do, and Text 2 describes it as being lighter in tone. However, while Text 1 calls <em>Orlando </em>an &ldquo;oddity&rdquo; and mentions that Woolf &ldquo;began it as a joke,&rdquo; Text 2 asserts that <em>Orlando </em>engages the same themes as Woolf&rsquo;s other great novels. Hence, the author of Text 2 would most likely accept that <em>Orlando </em>differs from Woolf&rsquo;s other novels but would also insist on its importance in the context of Woolf&rsquo;s work as a writer. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Text 2 does suggest that the humor in <em>Orlando </em>is effective. However, there&rsquo;s nothing in Text 2 to suggest that the author would agree that Woolf&rsquo;s talents were best suited to serious novels. Rather, the author of Text 2 compares <em>Orlando </em>favorably to other novels by Woolf that are implied to be darker in tone. Choice B is incorrect because the author of Text 2 does not indicate that <em>Orlando </em>is less impressive than Woolf&rsquo;s other novels, but instead points out that it engages the same themes as other novels by Woolf that are considered classics. Choice D is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in Text 1 or Text 2 to suggest that readers have generally ignored <em>Orlando </em>because of its reputation. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-023-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$49a2938f-42fc-4553-a427-1580bfdd1103$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6a1dc7c5","external_id":"d149ddec-9f97-4f22-a5b6-683f4090e8b9","vaultid":"2e3d15df-e144-45c4-97e9-52dfe2cc49f6","uId":"a24311f8-89b9-4200-b8ad-b21bebbdd922","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Virginia Woolf&rsquo;s 1928 novel <em>Orlando </em>is an oddity within her body of work. Her other major novels consist mainly of scenes of everyday life and describe their characters&rsquo; interior states in great detail, whereas <em>Orlando </em>propels itself through a series of fantastical events and considers its characters&rsquo; psychology more superficially. Woolf herself sometimes regarded the novel as a minor work, even admitting once that she &ldquo;began it as a joke.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Like Woolf&rsquo;s other great novels, <em>Orlando </em>portrays how people&rsquo;s memories inform their experience of the present. Like those works, it examines how people navigate social interactions shaped by gender and social class. Though it is lighter in tone&mdash;more entertaining, even&mdash;this literary &ldquo;joke&rdquo; nonetheless engages seriously with the themes that motivated the four or five other novels by Woolf that have achieved the status of literary classics.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the assessment of <em>Orlando </em>presented in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d707e9b6-c4cf-4952-b6d3-1b4143be8765","content":"<p>By conceding that Woolf&rsquo;s talents were best suited to serious novels but asserting that the humor in <em>Orlando </em>is often effective</p>"},{"id":"b8629449-dd1d-4a1c-ada3-a93c0a586252","content":"<p>By agreeing that <em>Orlando</em> is less impressive than certain other novels by Woolf but arguing that it should still be regarded as a classic</p>"},{"id":"c1ee30a0-9757-4740-875c-b01f2a00b354","content":"<p>By acknowledging that <em>Orlando</em> clearly differs from Woolf&rsquo;s other major novels but insisting on its centrality to her body of work nonetheless&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"88822456-967f-412a-99c2-ef9c49517b0b","content":"<p>By concurring that the reputation of <em>Orlando</em> as a minor work has led readers to overlook this novel but maintaining that the reputation is unearned&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["c1ee30a0-9757-4740-875c-b01f2a00b354"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the assessment of <em>Orlando </em>in Text 1. Both authors agree that <em>Orlando </em>is unusual for Woolf: Text 1 states that the novel examines its characters&rsquo; psychologies more superficially than Woolf&rsquo;s other novels do, and Text 2 describes it as being lighter in tone. However, while Text 1 calls <em>Orlando </em>an &ldquo;oddity&rdquo; and mentions that Woolf &ldquo;began it as a joke,&rdquo; Text 2 asserts that <em>Orlando </em>engages the same themes as Woolf&rsquo;s other great novels. Hence, the author of Text 2 would most likely accept that <em>Orlando </em>differs from Woolf&rsquo;s other novels but would also insist on its importance in the context of Woolf&rsquo;s work as a writer. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Text 2 does suggest that the humor in <em>Orlando </em>is effective. However, there&rsquo;s nothing in Text 2 to suggest that the author would agree that Woolf&rsquo;s talents were best suited to serious novels. Rather, the author of Text 2 compares <em>Orlando </em>favorably to other novels by Woolf that are implied to be darker in tone. Choice B is incorrect because the author of Text 2 does not indicate that <em>Orlando </em>is less impressive than Woolf&rsquo;s other novels, but instead points out that it engages the same themes as other novels by Woolf that are considered classics. Choice D is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in Text 1 or Text 2 to suggest that readers have generally ignored <em>Orlando </em>because of its reputation. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-023-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"49a2938f-42fc-4553-a427-1580bfdd1103","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$6ae393f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$1d40f21f-4afe-4105-8efe-b1cb7667042d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Asteroid 6478 Gault has experienced intermittent mass loss since at least 2013, but in contrast to some other asteroids with repeated mass-loss episodes, 6478 Gault has not lost mass at its perihelion (the closest point of its orbit to the Sun), and thus the loss is not attributable to solar energy&ndash;driven ice vaporization. And as Jane X. Luu et al. point out, the singular nature of impact ejection makes it untenable as an account of multiple loss episodes of similar duration over several years. Instead, Luu et al. are likely correct that 6478 Gault is shedding mass due to rotational instability.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"18268884-e455-47b8-9271-c7a83d21cdf8","content":"<p>It presents a scientific observation, describes a contrast between that observation and other observations, and then explains why those other observations should not be considered credible. &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"91176b27-2e0a-4f65-bc9f-16a9dfc3c631","content":"<p>It describes an astronomical finding, discusses competing theories about that finding that the author regards as flawed, and then describes new evidence that supports an alternative theory.</p>"},{"id":"2b19f9ff-de30-40d5-b6a0-9da31ce13cbc","content":"<p>It introduces a natural phenomenon, refutes two potential explanations for that phenomenon, and then presents a third explanation for that phenomenon that the author regards as plausible.</p>"},{"id":"16729b0c-f6b7-47d1-b413-6519fa85aa38","content":"<p>It discusses a physical process, evaluates possible causes of that process, and then states that a persuasive account of the process has yet to be put forward.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2b19f9ff-de30-40d5-b6a0-9da31ce13cbc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by introducing the natural phenomenon of intermittent mass loss with regard to Asteroid 6478 Gault and notes that it curiously has not lost mass at its perihelion like other asteroids typically do. The text then refutes "solar energy&ndash;driven ice vaporization" and "the singular nature of impact ejection" as two possible explanations for Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss. Finally, the text presents Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation that "6478 Gault is shedding mass due to rotational instability," which the author says is "likely correct." Thus, the overall structure of the text is that it introduces a natural phenomenon, refutes two potential explanations for that phenomenon, and then presents a third explanation for that phenomenon that the author regards as plausible.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t distinguish between multiple observations. It focuses on the single observation that Asteroid 6478 Gault is losing mass and evaluates multiple explanations for the phenomenon. The text also asserts that Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation is credible and "likely correct." Choice B is incorrect because the text describes a natural phenomenon, not a specific astronomical finding. In addition, although the text does note flawed reasons for Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss, it doesn&rsquo;t mention that Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss is based on new evidence. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text does evaluate explanations for the cause of Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss, the text also asserts that Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation that they have put forth is persuasive and "likely correct."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6ae393f7","external_id":"1d40f21f-4afe-4105-8efe-b1cb7667042d","vaultid":null,"uId":"a7022df3-d84a-409c-8d2a-e41dc46ef787","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Asteroid 6478 Gault has experienced intermittent mass loss since at least 2013, but in contrast to some other asteroids with repeated mass-loss episodes, 6478 Gault has not lost mass at its perihelion (the closest point of its orbit to the Sun), and thus the loss is not attributable to solar energy&ndash;driven ice vaporization. And as Jane X. Luu et al. point out, the singular nature of impact ejection makes it untenable as an account of multiple loss episodes of similar duration over several years. Instead, Luu et al. are likely correct that 6478 Gault is shedding mass due to rotational instability.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"18268884-e455-47b8-9271-c7a83d21cdf8","content":"<p>It presents a scientific observation, describes a contrast between that observation and other observations, and then explains why those other observations should not be considered credible. &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"91176b27-2e0a-4f65-bc9f-16a9dfc3c631","content":"<p>It describes an astronomical finding, discusses competing theories about that finding that the author regards as flawed, and then describes new evidence that supports an alternative theory.</p>"},{"id":"2b19f9ff-de30-40d5-b6a0-9da31ce13cbc","content":"<p>It introduces a natural phenomenon, refutes two potential explanations for that phenomenon, and then presents a third explanation for that phenomenon that the author regards as plausible.</p>"},{"id":"16729b0c-f6b7-47d1-b413-6519fa85aa38","content":"<p>It discusses a physical process, evaluates possible causes of that process, and then states that a persuasive account of the process has yet to be put forward.</p>"}],"keys":["2b19f9ff-de30-40d5-b6a0-9da31ce13cbc"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by introducing the natural phenomenon of intermittent mass loss with regard to Asteroid 6478 Gault and notes that it curiously has not lost mass at its perihelion like other asteroids typically do. The text then refutes \"solar energy&ndash;driven ice vaporization\" and \"the singular nature of impact ejection\" as two possible explanations for Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss. Finally, the text presents Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation that \"6478 Gault is shedding mass due to rotational instability,\" which the author says is \"likely correct.\" Thus, the overall structure of the text is that it introduces a natural phenomenon, refutes two potential explanations for that phenomenon, and then presents a third explanation for that phenomenon that the author regards as plausible.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t distinguish between multiple observations. It focuses on the single observation that Asteroid 6478 Gault is losing mass and evaluates multiple explanations for the phenomenon. The text also asserts that Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation is credible and \"likely correct.\" Choice B is incorrect because the text describes a natural phenomenon, not a specific astronomical finding. In addition, although the text does note flawed reasons for Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss, it doesn&rsquo;t mention that Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss is based on new evidence. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text does evaluate explanations for the cause of Asteroid 6478 Gault&rsquo;s intermittent mass loss, the text also asserts that Luu et al.&rsquo;s explanation that they have put forth is persuasive and \"likely correct.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$6bc0ba75$SATQ$, $SATQ$c05cd444-d000-4a40-8bbc-7229da22f4f4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The mimosa tree evolved in East Asia, where the beetle <em>Bruchidius terrenus</em> preys on its seeds. In <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1785</span>, mimosa trees were introduced to North America, far from any <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="B">B.</span> terrenus</em>. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents.</u></span> Around 2001, <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="B">B.</span> terrenus</em> was introduced in southeastern North America near where botanist Shu-Mei Chang and colleagues had been monitoring mimosa trees. Within a year, 93 percent of the trees had been attacked by the beetles.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1b6e3124-af9d-4905-993d-23fa94c5eb03","content":"<p>It states the hypothesis that Chang and colleagues had set out to investigate using mimosa trees and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"1372dc73-cd63-485c-8e53-5b9a8481c41b","content":"<p>It presents a generalization that is exemplified by the discussion of the mimosa trees and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"4d42c844-5fd9-409c-ac1f-6ea96dc144fc","content":"<p>It provides context that clarifies why the species mentioned spread to new locations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"6a1d2f40-a952-43d9-b870-ee21b4d99cc2","content":"<p>It offers an alternative explanation for the findings of Chang and colleagues.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1372dc73-cd63-485c-8e53-5b9a8481c41b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the third sentence within the overall structure of the text. The third sentence makes a generalization, asserting that evolutionary links between predators and prey can persist across great expanses of time and distance. This generalization is exemplified by the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between mimosa trees and <em>B. terrenus</em> beetles. When mimosa trees were introduced to North America in 1785, no <em>B. terrenus</em> beetles were present, so the relationship between the trees and the beetles that exists in their native East Asia was disrupted. When the beetles were introduced to North America more than 200 years later, however, they quickly attacked mimosa trees, illustrating the generalization that links between predators and prey "can persist across centuries and continents."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the third sentence doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Chang and colleagues were investigating any hypothesis. According to the text, Chang and colleagues were simply monitoring mimosa trees when the beetles happened to be introduced to the area. Choice C is incorrect because the third sentence doesn&rsquo;t discuss any particular species, let alone the species mentioned elsewhere in the text, nor does the sentence explain why species spread to new locations. Choice D is incorrect because the third sentence offers a generalization about the relationship between predators and prey, not an explanation for the findings by Chang and colleagues that&rsquo;s an "alternative" to an explanation presented elsewhere in the text.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6bc0ba75","external_id":"c05cd444-d000-4a40-8bbc-7229da22f4f4","vaultid":null,"uId":"e8d48ed3-91c6-4fe8-acfb-3db993b286d5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The mimosa tree evolved in East Asia, where the beetle <em>Bruchidius terrenus</em> preys on its seeds. In <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1785</span>, mimosa trees were introduced to North America, far from any <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em>. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents.</u></span> Around 2001, <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em> was introduced in southeastern North America near where botanist Shu-Mei Chang and colleagues had been monitoring mimosa trees. Within a year, 93 percent of the trees had been attacked by the beetles.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1b6e3124-af9d-4905-993d-23fa94c5eb03","content":"<p>It states the hypothesis that Chang and colleagues had set out to investigate using mimosa trees and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"1372dc73-cd63-485c-8e53-5b9a8481c41b","content":"<p>It presents a generalization that is exemplified by the discussion of the mimosa trees and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> terrenus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"4d42c844-5fd9-409c-ac1f-6ea96dc144fc","content":"<p>It provides context that clarifies why the species mentioned spread to new locations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"6a1d2f40-a952-43d9-b870-ee21b4d99cc2","content":"<p>It offers an alternative explanation for the findings of Chang and colleagues.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["1372dc73-cd63-485c-8e53-5b9a8481c41b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the third sentence within the overall structure of the text. The third sentence makes a generalization, asserting that evolutionary links between predators and prey can persist across great expanses of time and distance. This generalization is exemplified by the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between mimosa trees and <em>B. terrenus</em> beetles. When mimosa trees were introduced to North America in 1785, no <em>B. terrenus</em> beetles were present, so the relationship between the trees and the beetles that exists in their native East Asia was disrupted. When the beetles were introduced to North America more than 200 years later, however, they quickly attacked mimosa trees, illustrating the generalization that links between predators and prey \"can persist across centuries and continents.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the third sentence doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Chang and colleagues were investigating any hypothesis. According to the text, Chang and colleagues were simply monitoring mimosa trees when the beetles happened to be introduced to the area. Choice C is incorrect because the third sentence doesn&rsquo;t discuss any particular species, let alone the species mentioned elsewhere in the text, nor does the sentence explain why species spread to new locations. Choice D is incorrect because the third sentence offers a generalization about the relationship between predators and prey, not an explanation for the findings by Chang and colleagues that&rsquo;s an \"alternative\" to an explanation presented elsewhere in the text.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959311,"updateDate":1691007959311}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959311, 1691007959311),
    ($SATQ$6c086e70$SATQ$, $SATQ$348803a2-877e-46b0-a441-f68a03ea9a54$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Businesses typically try to promote themselves, but research by Michael Luca, Abhishek Nagaraj, and Gauri Subramani suggests that some do not <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> their marketing. The researchers studied restaurants in Texas and found that those listed on a popular review website (which can be done at no cost) had revenue increases of 5 percent on average, but 33 percent of restaurants studied never listed on the site.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5c866347-4044-49b5-8719-859b77ecdd38","content":"<p>adorn</p>"},{"id":"a29f5343-c46b-4adc-8f6b-d137d86aa288","content":"<p>optimize</p>"},{"id":"57d0b04d-6b00-428e-ba82-558583eeb613","content":"<p>endorse</p>"},{"id":"877a7314-5487-49d3-8319-784234e0f3f6","content":"<p>counteract</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a29f5343-c46b-4adc-8f6b-d137d86aa288"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of business marketing practices. In this context, "optimize" means make the most effective possible use of available opportunities. The text indicates that though businesses typically try to promote themselves, they don&rsquo;t always make use of effective tools for doing so, pointing to research showing that 33 percent of restaurants failed to use a free listing service that increased revenue by 5 percent on average for restaurants that did use the service. The text therefore suggests that by foregoing a free and effective marketing tool, some businesses aren&rsquo;t making the best use of, or optimizing, marketing opportunities.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "adorn" would mean decorate or enhance the appearance of, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that by failing to use a free and effective promotional tool, some businesses aren&rsquo;t enhancing the appearance of their own marketing. The example in the text illustrates that some businesses aren&rsquo;t utilizing an effective marketing opportunity, not that they aren&rsquo;t improving the appearance of their marketing. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "endorse" would mean openly approve or recommend, and the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fact that some businesses have overlooked an effective marketing tool doesn&rsquo;t support the idea that those businesses therefore don&rsquo;t approve of their own marketing. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, saying that businesses don&rsquo;t "counteract" their marketing would suggest that they don&rsquo;t actively undermine it or make it ineffective. The text begins by stating that businesses typically try to promote themselves but then continues to imply that some businesses fail to take advantage of good opportunities to do so, citing the example of businesses that failed to use a free and effective marketing tool. Though it&rsquo;s possible that those businesses that did use the site therefore didn&rsquo;t unintentionally counteract their own marketing, the first sentence conveys that the text&rsquo;s focus is on things that some businesses do and that some businesses don&rsquo;t do when trying to promote themselves, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that any businesses would actively try to undermine their own marketing in pursuit of that goal.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6c086e70","external_id":"348803a2-877e-46b0-a441-f68a03ea9a54","vaultid":null,"uId":"0d134bef-8fd8-43fb-9528-685539814460","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Businesses typically try to promote themselves, but research by Michael Luca, Abhishek Nagaraj, and Gauri Subramani suggests that some do not <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> their marketing. The researchers studied restaurants in Texas and found that those listed on a popular review website (which can be done at no cost) had revenue increases of 5 percent on average, but 33 percent of restaurants studied never listed on the site.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5c866347-4044-49b5-8719-859b77ecdd38","content":"<p>adorn</p>"},{"id":"a29f5343-c46b-4adc-8f6b-d137d86aa288","content":"<p>optimize</p>"},{"id":"57d0b04d-6b00-428e-ba82-558583eeb613","content":"<p>endorse</p>"},{"id":"877a7314-5487-49d3-8319-784234e0f3f6","content":"<p>counteract</p>"}],"keys":["a29f5343-c46b-4adc-8f6b-d137d86aa288"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of business marketing practices. In this context, \"optimize\" means make the most effective possible use of available opportunities. The text indicates that though businesses typically try to promote themselves, they don&rsquo;t always make use of effective tools for doing so, pointing to research showing that 33 percent of restaurants failed to use a free listing service that increased revenue by 5 percent on average for restaurants that did use the service. The text therefore suggests that by foregoing a free and effective marketing tool, some businesses aren&rsquo;t making the best use of, or optimizing, marketing opportunities.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"adorn\" would mean decorate or enhance the appearance of, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that by failing to use a free and effective promotional tool, some businesses aren&rsquo;t enhancing the appearance of their own marketing. The example in the text illustrates that some businesses aren&rsquo;t utilizing an effective marketing opportunity, not that they aren&rsquo;t improving the appearance of their marketing. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"endorse\" would mean openly approve or recommend, and the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fact that some businesses have overlooked an effective marketing tool doesn&rsquo;t support the idea that those businesses therefore don&rsquo;t approve of their own marketing. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, saying that businesses don&rsquo;t \"counteract\" their marketing would suggest that they don&rsquo;t actively undermine it or make it ineffective. The text begins by stating that businesses typically try to promote themselves but then continues to imply that some businesses fail to take advantage of good opportunities to do so, citing the example of businesses that failed to use a free and effective marketing tool. Though it&rsquo;s possible that those businesses that did use the site therefore didn&rsquo;t unintentionally counteract their own marketing, the first sentence conveys that the text&rsquo;s focus is on things that some businesses do and that some businesses don&rsquo;t do when trying to promote themselves, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that any businesses would actively try to undermine their own marketing in pursuit of that goal.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$6c64a8f3$SATQ$, $SATQ$0d6410db-f8b9-48d2-8712-acc6993450ec$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ecologist Exequiel Ezcurra and colleagues found that the inhabitants of the Mexica empire used natural landmarks to track time with a high degree of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>. By observing the sun&rsquo;s position in relation to various points on the mountains surrounding the Basin of Mexico, the Mexica were able to precisely identify the dates when significant events such as solstices occurred.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7e1a0f86-bebc-4673-b1b6-160224513869","content":"<p>precariousness</p>"},{"id":"da4e56cb-904a-445a-b2f1-71a03a7dbd2b","content":"<p>exactitude</p>"},{"id":"a9805c95-e028-4940-8df0-fa8954eedd28","content":"<p>resilience</p>"},{"id":"8752969d-1a2a-4da1-b700-2e149e0eecc9","content":"<p>inconspicuousness</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["da4e56cb-904a-445a-b2f1-71a03a7dbd2b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the method the inhabitants of the Mexica empire used to track time. In this context, "exactitude" means precision. The text indicates that the Mexica used the sun&rsquo;s position relative to various natural landmarks to track the passage of time, explaining that such methods allowed the Mexica to accurately determine the dates of important events such as solstices. This context supports the idea that the Mexica people were able to track time with a high degree of precision, or exactitude.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context "precariousness" would mean instability, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the method used by the Mexica people to track time was unstable or produced unstable results. Rather, the text indicates that the method yielded precise results, allowing the Mexica to accurately determine the dates of significant events. Choice C is incorrect because in this context "resilience" would mean persistence, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the Mexica worked stubbornly in spite of obstacles in order to track time. Choice D is incorrect because in this context "inconspicuousness" would mean discreetness, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the Mexica wanted to track time without being noticed.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6c64a8f3","external_id":"0d6410db-f8b9-48d2-8712-acc6993450ec","vaultid":null,"uId":"d3122ea5-45be-4c04-ad47-dd3e6508f02c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ecologist Exequiel Ezcurra and colleagues found that the inhabitants of the Mexica empire used natural landmarks to track time with a high degree of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>. By observing the sun&rsquo;s position in relation to various points on the mountains surrounding the Basin of Mexico, the Mexica were able to precisely identify the dates when significant events such as solstices occurred.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7e1a0f86-bebc-4673-b1b6-160224513869","content":"<p>precariousness</p>"},{"id":"da4e56cb-904a-445a-b2f1-71a03a7dbd2b","content":"<p>exactitude</p>"},{"id":"a9805c95-e028-4940-8df0-fa8954eedd28","content":"<p>resilience</p>"},{"id":"8752969d-1a2a-4da1-b700-2e149e0eecc9","content":"<p>inconspicuousness</p>"}],"keys":["da4e56cb-904a-445a-b2f1-71a03a7dbd2b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the method the inhabitants of the Mexica empire used to track time. In this context, \"exactitude\" means precision. The text indicates that the Mexica used the sun&rsquo;s position relative to various natural landmarks to track the passage of time, explaining that such methods allowed the Mexica to accurately determine the dates of important events such as solstices. This context supports the idea that the Mexica people were able to track time with a high degree of precision, or exactitude.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context \"precariousness\" would mean instability, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the method used by the Mexica people to track time was unstable or produced unstable results. Rather, the text indicates that the method yielded precise results, allowing the Mexica to accurately determine the dates of significant events. Choice C is incorrect because in this context \"resilience\" would mean persistence, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the Mexica worked stubbornly in spite of obstacles in order to track time. Choice D is incorrect because in this context \"inconspicuousness\" would mean discreetness, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the Mexica wanted to track time without being noticed.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323052,"updateDate":1730147323052}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323052, 1730147323052),
    ($SATQ$6c9f1727$SATQ$, $SATQ$ee427475-700a-4123-96a2-c2ab10dc89a9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>One popular theory of the origin of the Moon, the &ldquo;big whack,&rdquo; posits that a protoplanet called Theia collided with Earth, flinging debris into orbit that eventually coalesced into the Moon. Until recently, Theia was <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, but researcher Qian Yuan and colleagues now claim to have identified pieces of the protoplanet in the lowermost section of Earth&rsquo;s mantle.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e582491-0fd5-4a23-ae66-db1743fb4e97","content":"<p>desultory</p>"},{"id":"6400689b-6d01-4b8f-8c66-81f90d99feb5","content":"<p>spurious</p>"},{"id":"6cd88e68-fde3-4daa-a976-c91b5fdbe11d","content":"<p>veritable</p>"},{"id":"d71b13e1-740f-4ca6-8a49-b46d48f926d4","content":"<p>notional</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d71b13e1-740f-4ca6-8a49-b46d48f926d4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Theia and the origin of the Moon. In this context, "notional" means theoretical or only an idea. The text indicates that although something was once true of Theia, the protoplanet one theory holds collided with Earth and created debris that became the Moon, researchers now claim to have identified pieces of it deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. In other words, having direct evidence of Theia is a new development. This context suggests that before evidence was found, Theia was only theoretical&mdash;that is, that it was notional.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Theia, a protoplanet, was "desultory," which in this context would mean moving away from the matter at hand; indeed, the text focuses on the "big whack" theory of the origin of the Moon and indicates that Theia is a central part of that theory. Choice B is incorrect because the text makes the point that something was true of Theia until recently and suggests that this has changed now that researchers believe they have found pieces of the protoplanet deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Theia was actually "spurious," or false, until researchers found direct evidence of the protoplanet. (Although Theia&rsquo;s existence might not have been certain, it would not have been false simply because there was no evidence of it.) Choice C is incorrect because the text makes the point that something was true of Theia until recently and suggests that this has changed now that researchers believe they have found pieces of the protoplanet deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Theia was "veritable," or real, before researchers found direct evidence of its existence: the evidence would instead confirm that Theia was real.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6c9f1727","external_id":"ee427475-700a-4123-96a2-c2ab10dc89a9","vaultid":null,"uId":"0e23d182-5c4e-4626-a6f6-948cb0dc4416","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>One popular theory of the origin of the Moon, the &ldquo;big whack,&rdquo; posits that a protoplanet called Theia collided with Earth, flinging debris into orbit that eventually coalesced into the Moon. Until recently, Theia was <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, but researcher Qian Yuan and colleagues now claim to have identified pieces of the protoplanet in the lowermost section of Earth&rsquo;s mantle.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e582491-0fd5-4a23-ae66-db1743fb4e97","content":"<p>desultory</p>"},{"id":"6400689b-6d01-4b8f-8c66-81f90d99feb5","content":"<p>spurious</p>"},{"id":"6cd88e68-fde3-4daa-a976-c91b5fdbe11d","content":"<p>veritable</p>"},{"id":"d71b13e1-740f-4ca6-8a49-b46d48f926d4","content":"<p>notional</p>"}],"keys":["d71b13e1-740f-4ca6-8a49-b46d48f926d4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Theia and the origin of the Moon. In this context, \"notional\" means theoretical or only an idea. The text indicates that although something was once true of Theia, the protoplanet one theory holds collided with Earth and created debris that became the Moon, researchers now claim to have identified pieces of it deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. In other words, having direct evidence of Theia is a new development. This context suggests that before evidence was found, Theia was only theoretical&mdash;that is, that it was notional.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Theia, a protoplanet, was \"desultory,\" which in this context would mean moving away from the matter at hand; indeed, the text focuses on the \"big whack\" theory of the origin of the Moon and indicates that Theia is a central part of that theory. Choice B is incorrect because the text makes the point that something was true of Theia until recently and suggests that this has changed now that researchers believe they have found pieces of the protoplanet deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Theia was actually \"spurious,\" or false, until researchers found direct evidence of the protoplanet. (Although Theia&rsquo;s existence might not have been certain, it would not have been false simply because there was no evidence of it.) Choice C is incorrect because the text makes the point that something was true of Theia until recently and suggests that this has changed now that researchers believe they have found pieces of the protoplanet deep in Earth&rsquo;s mantle. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Theia was \"veritable,\" or real, before researchers found direct evidence of its existence: the evidence would instead confirm that Theia was real.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$6cf906fc$SATQ$, $SATQ$ead8caf0-2120-44b4-ac53-a12d2e19856b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Jos&eacute; Mart&iacute;&rsquo;s 1891 poem &ldquo;At the Salon&rdquo; (translated by Cecil Charles in 1898). The speaker describes a painting featuring a female figure.</p>
<blockquote class="dap_poemexcerpt">
<p>From the sterile soil no ray</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Of hope in a blade of green,</p>
<p>Nor <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>sheltering</u></span> roof is seen&mdash;</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Though the skies are heavy and gray.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Ah, this is the woman fair</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Who stole away my heart</p>
<p>In the splendid halls of art</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Yester-e&rsquo;en as I wandered there!</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;sheltering&rdquo; most nearly mean?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"acd52f0b-52a1-4255-8ed8-77feab970087","content":"<p>Protective</p>"},{"id":"cd052242-b60c-40e5-bc6b-d4b65fb7f96e","content":"<p>Restrictive</p>"},{"id":"56e80f79-0a46-4234-bf9c-43128e870638","content":"<p>Affectionate</p>"},{"id":"c2297b3d-9cea-47ab-aea5-bf89ffce5f6a","content":"<p>Suspicious</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["acd52f0b-52a1-4255-8ed8-77feab970087"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "sheltering" most nearly means protective. In the text, the speaker describes a painting featuring a female figure. The speaker ultimately confesses to falling in love with the woman in the painting, who has "stole[n] away" the speaker&rsquo;s heart, but first the speaker describes elements of the painting&rsquo;s setting. These include sterile soil, barren fields with "no ray / Of hope in a blade of green"&mdash;a description that conveys a mood of melancholy and forlornness, possibly reflecting the speaker&rsquo;s own sadness at falling in love with an image that can&rsquo;t return the speaker&rsquo;s love. The text continues by stating that there&rsquo;s no "sheltering roof" in the painting, even though the "skies are heavy and gray," showing signs of an impending storm. Generally, a roof&rsquo;s intended purpose is to provide protection from the elements, and in this context, a roof would offer shelter and protection from the gathering storm.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that a roof would be restrictive&mdash;that is, that it would limit or restrain something in some way. Although a roof would provide some cover from stormy winds and rain, it wouldn&rsquo;t limit the wind and rain, only deflect them. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that if a "sheltering roof" were present in the painting, it would be affectionate, or loving and tender. In the text, the immediate context in which the word "sheltering" appears refers to the absence of a roof that would provide protection from the oncoming storm depicted in the painting, not to the idea that such a shelter would be loving and tender if it were present. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, suspicious would mean questionable or dubious, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that if any roofs were present in the painting, they would be questionable or cause any suspicion.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6cf906fc","external_id":"ead8caf0-2120-44b4-ac53-a12d2e19856b","vaultid":null,"uId":"4ff8ab79-c250-46cf-a4b3-fbe413557d77","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Jos&eacute; Mart&iacute;&rsquo;s 1891 poem &ldquo;At the Salon&rdquo; (translated by Cecil Charles in 1898). The speaker describes a painting featuring a female figure.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"dap_poemexcerpt\">\n<p>From the sterile soil no ray</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Of hope in a blade of green,</p>\n<p>Nor <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>sheltering</u></span> roof is seen&mdash;</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Though the skies are heavy and gray.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Ah, this is the woman fair</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Who stole away my heart</p>\n<p>In the splendid halls of art</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;Yester-e&rsquo;en as I wandered there!</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;sheltering&rdquo; most nearly mean?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"acd52f0b-52a1-4255-8ed8-77feab970087","content":"<p>Protective</p>"},{"id":"cd052242-b60c-40e5-bc6b-d4b65fb7f96e","content":"<p>Restrictive</p>"},{"id":"56e80f79-0a46-4234-bf9c-43128e870638","content":"<p>Affectionate</p>"},{"id":"c2297b3d-9cea-47ab-aea5-bf89ffce5f6a","content":"<p>Suspicious</p>"}],"keys":["acd52f0b-52a1-4255-8ed8-77feab970087"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"sheltering\" most nearly means protective. In the text, the speaker describes a painting featuring a female figure. The speaker ultimately confesses to falling in love with the woman in the painting, who has \"stole[n] away\" the speaker&rsquo;s heart, but first the speaker describes elements of the painting&rsquo;s setting. These include sterile soil, barren fields with \"no ray / Of hope in a blade of green\"&mdash;a description that conveys a mood of melancholy and forlornness, possibly reflecting the speaker&rsquo;s own sadness at falling in love with an image that can&rsquo;t return the speaker&rsquo;s love. The text continues by stating that there&rsquo;s no \"sheltering roof\" in the painting, even though the \"skies are heavy and gray,\" showing signs of an impending storm. Generally, a roof&rsquo;s intended purpose is to provide protection from the elements, and in this context, a roof would offer shelter and protection from the gathering storm.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that a roof would be restrictive&mdash;that is, that it would limit or restrain something in some way. Although a roof would provide some cover from stormy winds and rain, it wouldn&rsquo;t limit the wind and rain, only deflect them. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that if a \"sheltering roof\" were present in the painting, it would be affectionate, or loving and tender. In the text, the immediate context in which the word \"sheltering\" appears refers to the absence of a roof that would provide protection from the oncoming storm depicted in the painting, not to the idea that such a shelter would be loving and tender if it were present. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, suspicious would mean questionable or dubious, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that if any roofs were present in the painting, they would be questionable or cause any suspicion.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323035,"updateDate":1730147323035}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323035, 1730147323035),
    ($SATQ$6d44060a$SATQ$, $SATQ$c6fac08a-82e1-4c30-980e-a4ac745bd846$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$102b0b6c-7250-4a10-af77-64596c3cef61$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Works of moral philosophy, such as Plato&rsquo;s <em>Republic</em> or Aristotle&rsquo;s <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, are partly concerned with how to live a morally good life. But philosopher Jonathan Barnes argues that works that present a method of living such a life without also supplying a motive are inherently useful only to those already wishing to be morally good&mdash;those with no desire for moral goodness will not choose to follow their rules. However, some works of moral philosophy attempt to describe what constitutes a morally good life while also proposing reasons for living one.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1c9b7445-1c54-4cd1-a919-4b75118f3ad7","content":"<p>It provides a characterization about a field of thought by noting two works in it and then details a way in which some works in that field are more comprehensive than others.</p>"},{"id":"7a801321-e470-447f-9b95-2720938d6a6e","content":"<p>It mentions two renowned works and then claims that despite their popularity it is impossible for these works to serve the purpose their authors intended.</p>"},{"id":"4b1e3ac0-8906-4bae-93c3-387474b02f6e","content":"<p>It summarizes the history of a field of thought by discussing two works and then proposes a topic of further research for specialists in that field.</p>"},{"id":"dc9671c8-2f6a-47e0-8501-ad4021c28199","content":"<p>It describes two influential works and then explains why one is more widely read than the other.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1c9b7445-1c54-4cd1-a919-4b75118f3ad7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The text starts by stating what moral philosophy is concerned with and naming two examples of works in the field. Then it describes a shortcoming of some works in that field (they say how but not why), and finally it states that other works try to avoid that shortcoming (by including both how and why to live a morally good life). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is too extreme. The text never mentions whether the two works are popular or not, and it never argues that these works don&rsquo;t serve their intended purpose of describing how to live a morally good life. Rather, the text claims that works of moral philosophy that don&rsquo;t include both how and why to be moral are not useful to readers who don&rsquo;t already want to be moral. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text never discusses the history of moral philosophy at all, and it doesn&rsquo;t propose any topic for further research. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text never discusses which of the two works is more widely read. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-H002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6daca9a5-9962-41e6-8cb3-e6c04005402a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6d44060a","external_id":"c6fac08a-82e1-4c30-980e-a4ac745bd846","vaultid":"102b0b6c-7250-4a10-af77-64596c3cef61","uId":"cd905936-a52a-48db-b822-2f9d35bfa472","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Works of moral philosophy, such as Plato&rsquo;s <em>Republic</em> or Aristotle&rsquo;s <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, are partly concerned with how to live a morally good life. But philosopher Jonathan Barnes argues that works that present a method of living such a life without also supplying a motive are inherently useful only to those already wishing to be morally good&mdash;those with no desire for moral goodness will not choose to follow their rules. However, some works of moral philosophy attempt to describe what constitutes a morally good life while also proposing reasons for living one.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1c9b7445-1c54-4cd1-a919-4b75118f3ad7","content":"<p>It provides a characterization about a field of thought by noting two works in it and then details a way in which some works in that field are more comprehensive than others.</p>"},{"id":"7a801321-e470-447f-9b95-2720938d6a6e","content":"<p>It mentions two renowned works and then claims that despite their popularity it is impossible for these works to serve the purpose their authors intended.</p>"},{"id":"4b1e3ac0-8906-4bae-93c3-387474b02f6e","content":"<p>It summarizes the history of a field of thought by discussing two works and then proposes a topic of further research for specialists in that field.</p>"},{"id":"dc9671c8-2f6a-47e0-8501-ad4021c28199","content":"<p>It describes two influential works and then explains why one is more widely read than the other.</p>"}],"keys":["1c9b7445-1c54-4cd1-a919-4b75118f3ad7"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The text starts by stating what moral philosophy is concerned with and naming two examples of works in the field. Then it describes a shortcoming of some works in that field (they say how but not why), and finally it states that other works try to avoid that shortcoming (by including both how and why to live a morally good life). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is too extreme. The text never mentions whether the two works are popular or not, and it never argues that these works don&rsquo;t serve their intended purpose of describing how to live a morally good life. Rather, the text claims that works of moral philosophy that don&rsquo;t include both how and why to be moral are not useful to readers who don&rsquo;t already want to be moral. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text never discusses the history of moral philosophy at all, and it doesn&rsquo;t propose any topic for further research. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text never discusses which of the two works is more widely read. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-H002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6daca9a5-9962-41e6-8cb3-e6c04005402a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$6d5ddea4$SATQ$, $SATQ$7f5faea8-d474-4411-8cdf-c80ad4475330$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$69ad93e0-1261-42e3-9bba-114cbeae6816$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to Potawatomi ecologist Robin Wall Kimmerer, the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, or sweetgrass, by snapping the plant off at the root actually <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> wild populations: it may seem counterintuitive, she says, but this method of removal allows new sweetgrass plants to repopulate the space, with an overall increase in number and vigor.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"14154ced-9e6c-4b6f-adb3-cb9db45c585d","content":"<p>selects</p>"},{"id":"feade69e-c192-49f8-a350-e5b534d3624c","content":"<p>originates</p>"},{"id":"13200fdf-5e6d-4ca7-ba77-ba9c5dd5d714","content":"<p>conditions</p>"},{"id":"1aadbd97-9dbf-41e8-9fa9-f17974e9440c","content":"<p>replenishes</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1aadbd97-9dbf-41e8-9fa9-f17974e9440c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata.</em> As used in this context, &ldquo;replenishes&rdquo; means helps increase the population or helps it recover. The text explains that although snapping off a wild plant at the root might seem detrimental to the wild population, it actually helps <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, increasing both their &ldquo;number and vigor.&rdquo; This context conveys the idea that even though it seems counterintuitive, the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata</em> actually replenishes the wild population. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although a harvesting method could be used to select for certain traits in plants, it&rsquo;s not clear what it would mean for a harvesting method to select &ldquo;wild populations&rdquo; of plants. Choice B is incorrect because as used in this context, &ldquo;originates&rdquo; means creates. The text doesn&rsquo;t address the origin of <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, but rather how the Indigenous harvesting method affects it. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;conditions&rdquo; means to influence someone or something to behave in a certain way, and the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest the new plants that replace the harvested ones differ in any meaningful way, or in any way that could be the result of conditioning. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-954-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$54b872b6-ea63-4c56-a60f-73d6ae7fa603$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6d5ddea4","external_id":"7f5faea8-d474-4411-8cdf-c80ad4475330","vaultid":"69ad93e0-1261-42e3-9bba-114cbeae6816","uId":"31d3bb23-7777-4ac1-848f-bad16dc2f5b7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to Potawatomi ecologist Robin Wall Kimmerer, the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, or sweetgrass, by snapping the plant off at the root actually <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> wild populations: it may seem counterintuitive, she says, but this method of removal allows new sweetgrass plants to repopulate the space, with an overall increase in number and vigor.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"14154ced-9e6c-4b6f-adb3-cb9db45c585d","content":"<p>selects</p>"},{"id":"feade69e-c192-49f8-a350-e5b534d3624c","content":"<p>originates</p>"},{"id":"13200fdf-5e6d-4ca7-ba77-ba9c5dd5d714","content":"<p>conditions</p>"},{"id":"1aadbd97-9dbf-41e8-9fa9-f17974e9440c","content":"<p>replenishes</p>"}],"keys":["1aadbd97-9dbf-41e8-9fa9-f17974e9440c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata.</em> As used in this context, &ldquo;replenishes&rdquo; means helps increase the population or helps it recover. The text explains that although snapping off a wild plant at the root might seem detrimental to the wild population, it actually helps <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, increasing both their &ldquo;number and vigor.&rdquo; This context conveys the idea that even though it seems counterintuitive, the Indigenous method of harvesting <em>Hierochloe odorata</em> actually replenishes the wild population. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although a harvesting method could be used to select for certain traits in plants, it&rsquo;s not clear what it would mean for a harvesting method to select &ldquo;wild populations&rdquo; of plants. Choice B is incorrect because as used in this context, &ldquo;originates&rdquo; means creates. The text doesn&rsquo;t address the origin of <em>Hierochloe odorata</em>, but rather how the Indigenous harvesting method affects it. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;conditions&rdquo; means to influence someone or something to behave in a certain way, and the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest the new plants that replace the harvested ones differ in any meaningful way, or in any way that could be the result of conditioning. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-954-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"54b872b6-ea63-4c56-a60f-73d6ae7fa603","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$6e3fdd1a$SATQ$, $SATQ$3e2440f0-a872-4575-a256-8df5dd1a9aeb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>During film&rsquo;s early years, those who worked in the industry had a vested interest in convincing the public to embrace the new medium. As Sumiko Higashi argues, some filmmakers relied on film critics to influence the public&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the world of cinema. Critics who drew similarities between film and traditional art forms, like drama, could help legitimize film as an art form.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"771a9cdc-daa1-4490-ba3f-fb868638a3c0","content":"<p>reproduction of</p>"},{"id":"14a17e73-bfa4-4814-9fd3-e387ac1f5b59","content":"<p>contribution to</p>"},{"id":"4d4f6b9e-7a90-4d9f-8b25-e075c37f0008","content":"<p>application to</p>"},{"id":"5c99318d-7910-4ef1-87a4-05b04df56da7","content":"<p>perception of</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5c99318d-7910-4ef1-87a4-05b04df56da7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the early years of film. In this context, &ldquo;perception of&rdquo; means understanding of or view of. The text states that people in the film industry had a personal stake (&ldquo;a vested interest&rdquo;) in getting the public to embrace film when the medium was new and indicates that some film critics helped legitimize film as an art form&mdash;that is, that they helped to make film something people accepted as a type of art. This context conveys that it was the critics&rsquo; ability to influence the public&rsquo;s view of the world of cinema that some early filmmakers relied on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;reproduction of&rdquo; would refer to a copy of something or the act of copying something, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to describe the general public as reproducing the world of cinema, or copying film as an entire art form. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;contribution to&rdquo; would refer to playing a significant part in causing something to happen. Although the text indicates that people in the early film industry had reasons to want the public to embrace film, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that filmmakers relied on film critics to influence how the public contributed to the world of cinema; the text focuses on the public&rsquo;s view of film once the world of cinema existed (embracing it, regarding it as legitimate art) and doesn&rsquo;t suggest the general public was responsible for the medium&rsquo;s existence. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;application to&rdquo; would refer to putting something to direct use, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the general public needed to be used in a practical way in the world of cinema. The text focuses on the importance of the public&rsquo;s view of film when the medium was new but not on the idea that the public needed to be directly involved in film.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6e3fdd1a","external_id":"3e2440f0-a872-4575-a256-8df5dd1a9aeb","vaultid":null,"uId":"580004ee-edb1-48e1-a93a-05e017a2f799","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>During film&rsquo;s early years, those who worked in the industry had a vested interest in convincing the public to embrace the new medium. As Sumiko Higashi argues, some filmmakers relied on film critics to influence the public&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the world of cinema. Critics who drew similarities between film and traditional art forms, like drama, could help legitimize film as an art form.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"771a9cdc-daa1-4490-ba3f-fb868638a3c0","content":"<p>reproduction of</p>"},{"id":"14a17e73-bfa4-4814-9fd3-e387ac1f5b59","content":"<p>contribution to</p>"},{"id":"4d4f6b9e-7a90-4d9f-8b25-e075c37f0008","content":"<p>application to</p>"},{"id":"5c99318d-7910-4ef1-87a4-05b04df56da7","content":"<p>perception of</p>"}],"keys":["5c99318d-7910-4ef1-87a4-05b04df56da7"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the early years of film. In this context, &ldquo;perception of&rdquo; means understanding of or view of. The text states that people in the film industry had a personal stake (&ldquo;a vested interest&rdquo;) in getting the public to embrace film when the medium was new and indicates that some film critics helped legitimize film as an art form&mdash;that is, that they helped to make film something people accepted as a type of art. This context conveys that it was the critics&rsquo; ability to influence the public&rsquo;s view of the world of cinema that some early filmmakers relied on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;reproduction of&rdquo; would refer to a copy of something or the act of copying something, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to describe the general public as reproducing the world of cinema, or copying film as an entire art form. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;contribution to&rdquo; would refer to playing a significant part in causing something to happen. Although the text indicates that people in the early film industry had reasons to want the public to embrace film, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that filmmakers relied on film critics to influence how the public contributed to the world of cinema; the text focuses on the public&rsquo;s view of film once the world of cinema existed (embracing it, regarding it as legitimate art) and doesn&rsquo;t suggest the general public was responsible for the medium&rsquo;s existence. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;application to&rdquo; would refer to putting something to direct use, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the general public needed to be used in a practical way in the world of cinema. The text focuses on the importance of the public&rsquo;s view of film when the medium was new but not on the idea that the public needed to be directly involved in film.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771168}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771168),
    ($SATQ$6f5fc289$SATQ$, $SATQ$0521c2e9-08c3-4a63-b9d0-3a9cf68944da$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8e4920d3-9da3-4b9e-874c-1ff565777fa6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Charles Dickens&rsquo;s 1854 novel <em>Hard Times</em>. Coketown is a fictional town in England.&nbsp;</p>
<p>[Coketown] contained several large streets all very like one another, and many small streets still more like one another, inhabited by people equally like one another, who all went in and out at the same hours, with the same sound upon the same pavements, to do the same work, and to whom every day was the same as yesterday and tomorrow, and every year the counterpart of the last and the next.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"91a40352-2f12-4f4c-84c1-d150a2f019b8","content":"<p>To emphasize the uniformity of both the town and the people who live there</p>"},{"id":"944ee768-c2d8-4d28-befc-161c5e5ba7f5","content":"<p>To explain the limited work opportunities available to the town&rsquo;s residents</p>"},{"id":"b9553ccc-02af-4c24-9242-8986abfce8b4","content":"<p>To reveal how the predictability of the town makes it easy for people lose track of time</p>"},{"id":"dc829cc3-7d49-44c8-947b-480965374bf6","content":"<p>To argue that the simplicity of life in the town makes it a pleasant place to live</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["91a40352-2f12-4f4c-84c1-d150a2f019b8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The author describes Coketown as having streets that are all very similar and residents who live similarly and do the same work. This repetition of similarities emphasizes how everything in Coketown is alike. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the text mentions that all the residents &ldquo;do the same work,&rdquo; it never explains what that work is or why everyone does it. Besides, the idea that they all do the same work is just one of several similarities among the townspeople described in the text. Choice C is incorrect. While the last sentence states that &ldquo;every day was the same as yesterday and tomorrow, and every year the counterpart of the last and the next,&rdquo; it never suggests that people actually &ldquo;lose track of time.&rdquo; This is also too narrow to be the main idea, since time is just one of many aspects of Coketown that the text describes as always being the same. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions whether life is simple in Coketown, and the town sounds as though it&rsquo;s probably a pretty dull place to live, rather than a pleasant one. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0783aede-71f1-483c-8839-767f0b1755c6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6f5fc289","external_id":"0521c2e9-08c3-4a63-b9d0-3a9cf68944da","vaultid":"8e4920d3-9da3-4b9e-874c-1ff565777fa6","uId":"5cf14ea8-47ef-470c-8743-feee8cae657d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Charles Dickens&rsquo;s 1854 novel <em>Hard Times</em>. Coketown is a fictional town in England.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>[Coketown] contained several large streets all very like one another, and many small streets still more like one another, inhabited by people equally like one another, who all went in and out at the same hours, with the same sound upon the same pavements, to do the same work, and to whom every day was the same as yesterday and tomorrow, and every year the counterpart of the last and the next.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"91a40352-2f12-4f4c-84c1-d150a2f019b8","content":"<p>To emphasize the uniformity of both the town and the people who live there</p>"},{"id":"944ee768-c2d8-4d28-befc-161c5e5ba7f5","content":"<p>To explain the limited work opportunities available to the town&rsquo;s residents</p>"},{"id":"b9553ccc-02af-4c24-9242-8986abfce8b4","content":"<p>To reveal how the predictability of the town makes it easy for people lose track of time</p>"},{"id":"dc829cc3-7d49-44c8-947b-480965374bf6","content":"<p>To argue that the simplicity of life in the town makes it a pleasant place to live</p>"}],"keys":["91a40352-2f12-4f4c-84c1-d150a2f019b8"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The author describes Coketown as having streets that are all very similar and residents who live similarly and do the same work. This repetition of similarities emphasizes how everything in Coketown is alike. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the text mentions that all the residents &ldquo;do the same work,&rdquo; it never explains what that work is or why everyone does it. Besides, the idea that they all do the same work is just one of several similarities among the townspeople described in the text. Choice C is incorrect. While the last sentence states that &ldquo;every day was the same as yesterday and tomorrow, and every year the counterpart of the last and the next,&rdquo; it never suggests that people actually &ldquo;lose track of time.&rdquo; This is also too narrow to be the main idea, since time is just one of many aspects of Coketown that the text describes as always being the same. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions whether life is simple in Coketown, and the town sounds as though it&rsquo;s probably a pretty dull place to live, rather than a pleasant one. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0783aede-71f1-483c-8839-767f0b1755c6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$70612a79$SATQ$, $SATQ$3404cd91-3bc8-4067-91ef-f45e0f508b0b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Following the eradication of the gray wolf in Yellowstone National Park in 1926, the population of elk&mdash;a primary prey of the gray wolf&mdash;exceeded a healthy size for the park&rsquo;s ecosystem. Elk overpopulation led to overgrazing of areas that a multitude of other animals relied on for food and shelter. As scientists began to see how essential the gray wolf was to the Yellowstone food chain, ecological restoration strategies were employed to reintroduce the gray wolf to the park in 1996. The rebound effect in the park&rsquo;s natural ecosystem was noticed almost immediately.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"88a0738a-fc3c-4f71-a52a-37c5f5ff0f5a","content":"<p>It summarizes a problem that developed in Yellowstone National Park in the 1920s and then offers potential solutions to that problem.</p>"},{"id":"eed7e96d-fcd7-4c33-b214-002e27978245","content":"<p>It mentions the elimination of the gray wolf from Yellowstone National Park and then explains why the wolf was eventually restored to the park.</p>"},{"id":"d563648b-de30-4708-8ee8-d9d52458d7fe","content":"<p>It presents a claim about the health of the Yellowstone National Park gray wolf population and then gives specific examples to support that claim.</p>"},{"id":"e3cefe99-51a9-4ff8-ad16-f975ca645b2e","content":"<p>It explains why Yellowstone National Park allowed the eradication of the gray wolf and then discusses the consequences of reintroducing the wolf to the park.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eed7e96d-fcd7-4c33-b214-002e27978245"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by stating that the gray wolf was eradicated from Yellowstone National Park in 1926. It then explains the consequences of this elimination&mdash;namely that the elk population grew too large and overgrazing occurred, negatively affecting other animals in the area. The text then states that once scientists realized the importance of the gray wolf to the Yellowstone food chain, they undertook efforts leading to the wolves&rsquo; reintroduction to the park in 1996. Thus, the structure of the text can be accurately described as mentioning the elimination of the gray wolf from Yellowstone and then explaining why the wolf was later restored to the park.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the text does summarize a problem associated with an event in the 1920s (the eradication of gray wolves from Yellowstone in 1926), the problems discussed arose after that event&mdash;namely, the subsequent elk overpopulation, resultant overgrazing, and then further impacts to other animals in Yellowstone. Furthermore, the text doesn&rsquo;t present multiple potential solutions. Instead, it describes one solution that was actually implemented: the reintroduction of gray wolves in 1996. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t make a claim about the health of the gray wolf population apart from pointing out that the wolves had been eradicated from, and then subsequently restored to, Yellowstone. Instead, the text focuses on outcomes associated with the gray wolf&rsquo;s eradication and reintroduction. Choice D is incorrect. Though the text does mention that the ecosystem of Yellowstone rebounded after the gray wolf was reintroduced in 1996, the text never explains why the park allowed the wolves to be eradicated in 1926. It simply states that this eradication occurred without elaborating on the decision-making process behind it.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"70612a79","external_id":"3404cd91-3bc8-4067-91ef-f45e0f508b0b","vaultid":null,"uId":"8a84dfcb-4624-45a5-9e7d-cd1d7b2ab264","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Following the eradication of the gray wolf in Yellowstone National Park in 1926, the population of elk&mdash;a primary prey of the gray wolf&mdash;exceeded a healthy size for the park&rsquo;s ecosystem. Elk overpopulation led to overgrazing of areas that a multitude of other animals relied on for food and shelter. As scientists began to see how essential the gray wolf was to the Yellowstone food chain, ecological restoration strategies were employed to reintroduce the gray wolf to the park in 1996. The rebound effect in the park&rsquo;s natural ecosystem was noticed almost immediately.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"88a0738a-fc3c-4f71-a52a-37c5f5ff0f5a","content":"<p>It summarizes a problem that developed in Yellowstone National Park in the 1920s and then offers potential solutions to that problem.</p>"},{"id":"eed7e96d-fcd7-4c33-b214-002e27978245","content":"<p>It mentions the elimination of the gray wolf from Yellowstone National Park and then explains why the wolf was eventually restored to the park.</p>"},{"id":"d563648b-de30-4708-8ee8-d9d52458d7fe","content":"<p>It presents a claim about the health of the Yellowstone National Park gray wolf population and then gives specific examples to support that claim.</p>"},{"id":"e3cefe99-51a9-4ff8-ad16-f975ca645b2e","content":"<p>It explains why Yellowstone National Park allowed the eradication of the gray wolf and then discusses the consequences of reintroducing the wolf to the park.</p>"}],"keys":["eed7e96d-fcd7-4c33-b214-002e27978245"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by stating that the gray wolf was eradicated from Yellowstone National Park in 1926. It then explains the consequences of this elimination&mdash;namely that the elk population grew too large and overgrazing occurred, negatively affecting other animals in the area. The text then states that once scientists realized the importance of the gray wolf to the Yellowstone food chain, they undertook efforts leading to the wolves&rsquo; reintroduction to the park in 1996. Thus, the structure of the text can be accurately described as mentioning the elimination of the gray wolf from Yellowstone and then explaining why the wolf was later restored to the park.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the text does summarize a problem associated with an event in the 1920s (the eradication of gray wolves from Yellowstone in 1926), the problems discussed arose after that event&mdash;namely, the subsequent elk overpopulation, resultant overgrazing, and then further impacts to other animals in Yellowstone. Furthermore, the text doesn&rsquo;t present multiple potential solutions. Instead, it describes one solution that was actually implemented: the reintroduction of gray wolves in 1996. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t make a claim about the health of the gray wolf population apart from pointing out that the wolves had been eradicated from, and then subsequently restored to, Yellowstone. Instead, the text focuses on outcomes associated with the gray wolf&rsquo;s eradication and reintroduction. Choice D is incorrect. Though the text does mention that the ecosystem of Yellowstone rebounded after the gray wolf was reintroduced in 1996, the text never explains why the park allowed the wolves to be eradicated in 1926. It simply states that this eradication occurred without elaborating on the decision-making process behind it.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771168}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771168),
    ($SATQ$710799ae$SATQ$, $SATQ$e6a89c32-d946-45e3-a460-50ba2c3c8868$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> at exercising self-control: in a 2021 study conducted by behavioral ecologist Alexandra Schnell, these cephalopods routinely demonstrated restraint by delaying gratification, waiting for a favorite treat instead of instantly devouring a readily available meal.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef9c24f0-cb5b-4413-89cc-07d4981aad63","content":"<p>awkward</p>"},{"id":"de07d830-ce60-48c1-9177-d81e9e6dd5fd","content":"<p>imaginative</p>"},{"id":"b48473b4-483c-4645-90d3-b012ee048a17","content":"<p>manageable</p>"},{"id":"77fb4d11-653f-4143-b81b-9c969e14b061","content":"<p>competent</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["77fb4d11-653f-4143-b81b-9c969e14b061"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of self-control in cuttlefish. In this context, "competent" means capable. The text describes a study in which cuttlefish demonstrated an ability to exercise restraint by waiting for a favorite treat instead of eating a meal that was already available to them. That is, by delaying gratification, the cuttlefish in the study demonstrated that they were capable of, or competent at, exercising self-control.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that in demonstrating self-control, cuttlefish are "awkward," or lacking skill or grace. If anything, the text indicates that cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly skilled at exercising self-control. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, "imaginative" would mean creative, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that cuttlefish demonstrate creativity. Instead, the text focuses on the fact that cuttlefish seem to be capable of exercising restraint and self-control. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "manageable" would mean capable of being controlled. Although the text indicates that the cuttlefish in the study demonstrated restraint by waiting for a favorite treat instead of devouring a readily available meal (which suggests their ability to control themselves), it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that cuttlefish are thus also able to be managed or controlled by others.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"710799ae","external_id":"e6a89c32-d946-45e3-a460-50ba2c3c8868","vaultid":null,"uId":"20cd8205-31e5-4bf0-b252-1135f9ce6683","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> at exercising self-control: in a 2021 study conducted by behavioral ecologist Alexandra Schnell, these cephalopods routinely demonstrated restraint by delaying gratification, waiting for a favorite treat instead of instantly devouring a readily available meal.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef9c24f0-cb5b-4413-89cc-07d4981aad63","content":"<p>awkward</p>"},{"id":"de07d830-ce60-48c1-9177-d81e9e6dd5fd","content":"<p>imaginative</p>"},{"id":"b48473b4-483c-4645-90d3-b012ee048a17","content":"<p>manageable</p>"},{"id":"77fb4d11-653f-4143-b81b-9c969e14b061","content":"<p>competent</p>"}],"keys":["77fb4d11-653f-4143-b81b-9c969e14b061"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of self-control in cuttlefish. In this context, \"competent\" means capable. The text describes a study in which cuttlefish demonstrated an ability to exercise restraint by waiting for a favorite treat instead of eating a meal that was already available to them. That is, by delaying gratification, the cuttlefish in the study demonstrated that they were capable of, or competent at, exercising self-control.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that in demonstrating self-control, cuttlefish are \"awkward,\" or lacking skill or grace. If anything, the text indicates that cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly skilled at exercising self-control. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, \"imaginative\" would mean creative, and there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that cuttlefish demonstrate creativity. Instead, the text focuses on the fact that cuttlefish seem to be capable of exercising restraint and self-control. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"manageable\" would mean capable of being controlled. Although the text indicates that the cuttlefish in the study demonstrated restraint by waiting for a favorite treat instead of devouring a readily available meal (which suggests their ability to control themselves), it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that cuttlefish are thus also able to be managed or controlled by others.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$74446089$SATQ$, $SATQ$0e72b9ee-4ae3-4961-9f29-27da9fd2c52e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9e0a2c3c-53a3-4938-902c-36ee1f2940a4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>For his 1986 album <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, Beverly Glenn-Copeland wrote songs grounded in traditional soul and folk music, then accompanied them with futuristic synthesizer arrangements featuring ambient sounds and complex rhythms. The result was so strange, so unprecedented, that the album attracted little attention when first released. In recent years, however, a younger generation of musicians has embraced the stylistic experimentation of <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Alternative R&amp;B musicians Blood Orange and Moses Sumney, among other contemporary recording artists, cite the album as an influence.</u></span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e959b373-d124-430a-8ca5-4116f3170083","content":"<p>It urges contemporary musicians to adopt the unique sound of <em>Keyboard Fantasies.</em></p>"},{"id":"1080f186-05c7-4275-ac56-dc5ff5bf0a8e","content":"<p>It responds to criticism of <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> by some younger musicians.</p>"},{"id":"4b888cb4-9800-4546-85b8-72fd2414a686","content":"<p>It offers examples of younger musicians whose work has been impacted by <em>Keyboard Fantasies.</em></p>"},{"id":"1b1f526a-d42d-4b63-afa8-2ec8639d3201","content":"<p>It contrasts <em>Keyboard Fantasies </em>with the recordings of two younger musicians.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4b888cb4-9800-4546-85b8-72fd2414a686"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole. The text&rsquo;s subject is Beverly Glenn-Copeland&rsquo;s 1986 album <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, notable for its innovative, experimental arrangements. According to the text, the album was not initially admired, but in recent years it has become popular among younger musicians. The underlined portion of the text mentions two of those musicians, Blood Orange and Moses Sumney, who &ldquo;cite the album as an influence.&rdquo; Therefore, the underlined portion of the text offers examples of younger musicians whose work has been impacted by <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because even though the underlined sentence states that Blood Orange and Moses Sumney were influenced by <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t say that all other musicians should also embrace the album&rsquo;s experimental style. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text states that <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> was not admired on its first release, the text doesn&rsquo;t present any criticism of the album by younger musicians: it only presents two younger musicians who cite it as an influence. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t mention any differences between <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> and the work of Blood Orange and Moses Sumney. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-048-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a223495f-7b9c-4d19-96ec-2095b0ecea1c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"74446089","external_id":"0e72b9ee-4ae3-4961-9f29-27da9fd2c52e","vaultid":"9e0a2c3c-53a3-4938-902c-36ee1f2940a4","uId":"7bf9e792-28f1-417b-a375-b25cfccfd531","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>For his 1986 album <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, Beverly Glenn-Copeland wrote songs grounded in traditional soul and folk music, then accompanied them with futuristic synthesizer arrangements featuring ambient sounds and complex rhythms. The result was so strange, so unprecedented, that the album attracted little attention when first released. In recent years, however, a younger generation of musicians has embraced the stylistic experimentation of <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Alternative R&amp;B musicians Blood Orange and Moses Sumney, among other contemporary recording artists, cite the album as an influence.</u></span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e959b373-d124-430a-8ca5-4116f3170083","content":"<p>It urges contemporary musicians to adopt the unique sound of <em>Keyboard Fantasies.</em></p>"},{"id":"1080f186-05c7-4275-ac56-dc5ff5bf0a8e","content":"<p>It responds to criticism of <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> by some younger musicians.</p>"},{"id":"4b888cb4-9800-4546-85b8-72fd2414a686","content":"<p>It offers examples of younger musicians whose work has been impacted by <em>Keyboard Fantasies.</em></p>"},{"id":"1b1f526a-d42d-4b63-afa8-2ec8639d3201","content":"<p>It contrasts <em>Keyboard Fantasies </em>with the recordings of two younger musicians.</p>"}],"keys":["4b888cb4-9800-4546-85b8-72fd2414a686"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole. The text&rsquo;s subject is Beverly Glenn-Copeland&rsquo;s 1986 album <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, notable for its innovative, experimental arrangements. According to the text, the album was not initially admired, but in recent years it has become popular among younger musicians. The underlined portion of the text mentions two of those musicians, Blood Orange and Moses Sumney, who &ldquo;cite the album as an influence.&rdquo; Therefore, the underlined portion of the text offers examples of younger musicians whose work has been impacted by <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because even though the underlined sentence states that Blood Orange and Moses Sumney were influenced by <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t say that all other musicians should also embrace the album&rsquo;s experimental style. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text states that <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> was not admired on its first release, the text doesn&rsquo;t present any criticism of the album by younger musicians: it only presents two younger musicians who cite it as an influence. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t mention any differences between <em>Keyboard Fantasies</em> and the work of Blood Orange and Moses Sumney. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-048-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a223495f-7b9c-4d19-96ec-2095b0ecea1c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959323,"updateDate":1691007959323}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959323, 1691007959323),
    ($SATQ$749f3334$SATQ$, $SATQ$511211a7-f7ba-49f7-af08-8c075158a19c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$56028e3f-b6cc-4c37-979b-3cb61fb32bd6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Charlotte Forten Grimk&eacute;&rsquo;s 1888 poem &ldquo;At Newport.&rdquo;</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Oh, deep delight to watch the gladsome waves<br>Exultant leap upon the rugged rocks;<br><span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on&mdash;</u></span><br><span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Filled with a life that will not know defeat;</u></span><br>To see the glorious hues of sky and sea.<br>The distant snowy sails, glide spirit like,<br>Into an unknown world, to feel the sweet<br>Enchantment of the sea thrill all the soul,<br>Clearing the clouded brain, making the heart<br>Leap joyous as it own bright, singing waves!</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"602eeee8-e207-4373-9e2f-87cb1ddeaee0","content":"<p>It portrays the surroundings as an imposing and intimidating scene.</p>"},{"id":"fe51a1cf-1698-4e96-af04-d27db3f0831a","content":"<p>It characterizes the sea&rsquo;s waves as a relentless and enduring force.</p>"},{"id":"038c9403-513c-4fc0-8dd7-e37af6ccd30c","content":"<p>It conveys the speaker&rsquo;s ambivalence about the natural world.</p>"},{"id":"4533aa8d-9fa2-4144-a1af-81440507b60f","content":"<p>It draws a contrast between the sea&rsquo;s waves and the speaker&rsquo;s thoughts.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fe51a1cf-1698-4e96-af04-d27db3f0831a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The text presents the speaker&rsquo;s experience of viewing the sea. In the underlined portion, the speaker focuses on the idea that the waves hitting rocks on the shore are a relentless and enduring force: they are constantly pushed back (&ldquo;ever repulsed&rdquo;) but always return (&ldquo;ever rushing on&rdquo;), as though they have an energy that can&rsquo;t be overcome (&ldquo;a life that will not know defeat&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the underlined portion characterizes the waves as a relentless force (always &ldquo;repulsed&rdquo; but still &ldquo;rushing on&rdquo; and never being defeated), the speaker doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the surroundings are intimidating. Instead, the speaker presents the scene in a positive way, describing the &ldquo;deep delight&rdquo; of the &ldquo;gladsome,&rdquo; or cheerful, waves and feeling &ldquo;the heart / Leap joyous&rdquo; while viewing the sea. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the speaker is ambivalent, or has mixed feelings about, the natural world. Instead, it presents a single view of one part of the immediate surroundings: the speaker characterizes the sea&rsquo;s waves as an unstoppable force, since they are constantly pushed back but always return (&ldquo;ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on&rdquo;).&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text later suggests the speaker&rsquo;s view of her own thoughts by referring to a &ldquo;clouded brain&rdquo; and a heart that leaps joyously, this reference neither occurs within the underlined portion nor establishes a clear contrast with the relentless determination of the waves. The underlined portion addresses only the speaker&rsquo;s view of the waves and doesn&rsquo;t suggest what her own thoughts might be. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-019-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1f020530-86d8-4c98-861a-a72f67e08736$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"749f3334","external_id":"511211a7-f7ba-49f7-af08-8c075158a19c","vaultid":"56028e3f-b6cc-4c37-979b-3cb61fb32bd6","uId":"9e968857-b523-4000-87f3-1e967558647d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Charlotte Forten Grimk&eacute;&rsquo;s 1888 poem &ldquo;At Newport.&rdquo;</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Oh, deep delight to watch the gladsome waves<br>Exultant leap upon the rugged rocks;<br><span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on&mdash;</u></span><br><span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Filled with a life that will not know defeat;</u></span><br>To see the glorious hues of sky and sea.<br>The distant snowy sails, glide spirit like,<br>Into an unknown world, to feel the sweet<br>Enchantment of the sea thrill all the soul,<br>Clearing the clouded brain, making the heart<br>Leap joyous as it own bright, singing waves!</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"602eeee8-e207-4373-9e2f-87cb1ddeaee0","content":"<p>It portrays the surroundings as an imposing and intimidating scene.</p>"},{"id":"fe51a1cf-1698-4e96-af04-d27db3f0831a","content":"<p>It characterizes the sea&rsquo;s waves as a relentless and enduring force.</p>"},{"id":"038c9403-513c-4fc0-8dd7-e37af6ccd30c","content":"<p>It conveys the speaker&rsquo;s ambivalence about the natural world.</p>"},{"id":"4533aa8d-9fa2-4144-a1af-81440507b60f","content":"<p>It draws a contrast between the sea&rsquo;s waves and the speaker&rsquo;s thoughts.</p>"}],"keys":["fe51a1cf-1698-4e96-af04-d27db3f0831a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The text presents the speaker&rsquo;s experience of viewing the sea. In the underlined portion, the speaker focuses on the idea that the waves hitting rocks on the shore are a relentless and enduring force: they are constantly pushed back (&ldquo;ever repulsed&rdquo;) but always return (&ldquo;ever rushing on&rdquo;), as though they have an energy that can&rsquo;t be overcome (&ldquo;a life that will not know defeat&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the underlined portion characterizes the waves as a relentless force (always &ldquo;repulsed&rdquo; but still &ldquo;rushing on&rdquo; and never being defeated), the speaker doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the surroundings are intimidating. Instead, the speaker presents the scene in a positive way, describing the &ldquo;deep delight&rdquo; of the &ldquo;gladsome,&rdquo; or cheerful, waves and feeling &ldquo;the heart / Leap joyous&rdquo; while viewing the sea. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the speaker is ambivalent, or has mixed feelings about, the natural world. Instead, it presents a single view of one part of the immediate surroundings: the speaker characterizes the sea&rsquo;s waves as an unstoppable force, since they are constantly pushed back but always return (&ldquo;ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on&rdquo;).&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text later suggests the speaker&rsquo;s view of her own thoughts by referring to a &ldquo;clouded brain&rdquo; and a heart that leaps joyously, this reference neither occurs within the underlined portion nor establishes a clear contrast with the relentless determination of the waves. The underlined portion addresses only the speaker&rsquo;s view of the waves and doesn&rsquo;t suggest what her own thoughts might be. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-019-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1f020530-86d8-4c98-861a-a72f67e08736","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959316,"updateDate":1691007959316}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959316, 1691007959316),
    ($SATQ$74ee7737$SATQ$, $SATQ$290fa114-1e66-4edc-be91-af800775d355$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Whether Carmen Lomas Garza is creating small paintings and illustrations or large public artworks&mdash;such as <em>Baile</em>, a copper cutout of traditional Mexican dance in the San Francisco International Airport&mdash;she is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> direct experience, drawing from memories of her childhood in Texas or details of her current surroundings in California.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6f79d191-4241-409d-adac-267937af3b68","content":"<p>complimented by</p>"},{"id":"f4364f2c-a0d9-4796-9fe1-81be5e06e8f9","content":"<p>uncertain about</p>"},{"id":"c4617481-8e6e-4699-8e75-a190b6ed46e8","content":"<p>unbothered by</p>"},{"id":"bca98cdb-d4ae-4355-9fc2-1da611e23702","content":"<p>inspired by</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bca98cdb-d4ae-4355-9fc2-1da611e23702"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Carmen Lomas Garza&rsquo;s artistic process. In this context, "inspired by" means influenced by or motivated by. The text refers to how, regardless of the scale of the work, Garza uses her memories of Texas and details from California to create her art. If Garza is basing her work on her direct experiences, then they play a part in her artistic process. This context thus suggests that Garza&rsquo;s art is inspired by the experiences of her childhood in Texas and her current life in California.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make logical sense to indicate that Garza is "complimented by"&mdash;or praised by&mdash;something inanimate such as direct experience. Choice B is incorrect because describing Garza as "uncertain about"&mdash;or unsure or doubtful of&mdash;direct experience would suggest that she had misgivings about it. If Garza were unsure of her experiences, that would suggest that she couldn&rsquo;t recall them, and Garza wouldn&rsquo;t be able to represent direct experience in her art if she were uncertain of the memories or details. Choice C is incorrect because describing Garza as "unbothered by"&mdash;or uninterested in&mdash;her experience would imply the opposite of what the text suggests about Garza&rsquo;s artistic process. The text indicates that Garza&rsquo;s art comes from memories of her childhood in Texas and details of her surroundings in California.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"74ee7737","external_id":"290fa114-1e66-4edc-be91-af800775d355","vaultid":null,"uId":"61d189dc-63f0-4f9c-b461-fe6ad36aba3c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Whether Carmen Lomas Garza is creating small paintings and illustrations or large public artworks&mdash;such as <em>Baile</em>, a copper cutout of traditional Mexican dance in the San Francisco International Airport&mdash;she is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> direct experience, drawing from memories of her childhood in Texas or details of her current surroundings in California.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6f79d191-4241-409d-adac-267937af3b68","content":"<p>complimented by</p>"},{"id":"f4364f2c-a0d9-4796-9fe1-81be5e06e8f9","content":"<p>uncertain about</p>"},{"id":"c4617481-8e6e-4699-8e75-a190b6ed46e8","content":"<p>unbothered by</p>"},{"id":"bca98cdb-d4ae-4355-9fc2-1da611e23702","content":"<p>inspired by</p>"}],"keys":["bca98cdb-d4ae-4355-9fc2-1da611e23702"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Carmen Lomas Garza&rsquo;s artistic process. In this context, \"inspired by\" means influenced by or motivated by. The text refers to how, regardless of the scale of the work, Garza uses her memories of Texas and details from California to create her art. If Garza is basing her work on her direct experiences, then they play a part in her artistic process. This context thus suggests that Garza&rsquo;s art is inspired by the experiences of her childhood in Texas and her current life in California.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make logical sense to indicate that Garza is \"complimented by\"&mdash;or praised by&mdash;something inanimate such as direct experience. Choice B is incorrect because describing Garza as \"uncertain about\"&mdash;or unsure or doubtful of&mdash;direct experience would suggest that she had misgivings about it. If Garza were unsure of her experiences, that would suggest that she couldn&rsquo;t recall them, and Garza wouldn&rsquo;t be able to represent direct experience in her art if she were uncertain of the memories or details. Choice C is incorrect because describing Garza as \"unbothered by\"&mdash;or uninterested in&mdash;her experience would imply the opposite of what the text suggests about Garza&rsquo;s artistic process. The text indicates that Garza&rsquo;s art comes from memories of her childhood in Texas and details of her surroundings in California.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323035,"updateDate":1730147323035}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323035, 1730147323035)
